<meta name="pb-short-title" content="Social Work Statistics" />
<meta name="pb-subtitle" content="An Introduction to Statistics in Social Work Research and Practice" />
<meta name="pb-about-50" content="This book provides a foundational education of descriptive and inferential statistics that are used in social work and similar social science fields. Students will learn mathematical techniques used to organize, summarize, and interpret data from assessments, reports, research, and evaluations. This book emphasizes the application of statistical methods in social work research and practice through case studies and examples from other social work contexts." />
<meta name="pb-about-unlimited" content="Many social work students want to focus on clinical practice and are under the false assumption that they will never need to use research or statistics. However, even if students never conduct their own research as future clinicians, they still are using therapeutic modalities that have been (and continue to be) informed by research. Thus, have a foundation of statistics and research is critical for social workers. Social workers also may be asked to contribute to program or policy evaluations, score and interpret clinical assessment tools, modify programming materials to better fit their clients’ needs, or advocate for new policies and practices. Across all these roles of social workers, having a knowledge of statistics can help guide these activities.In this introductory book, students will learn the mathematical techniques that are used to organize, summarize, and interpret data obtained through assessments, reports, research, and evaluations. Students will learn foundational descriptive and inferential statistics that are used in social work and other similar social science fields. This book emphasizes the application of statistical methods in social work research and practice through case studies and examples from other social work contexts.Throughout the textbook, examples will be inclusive of diverse clients, participants, and communities, including folks from diverse racial and ethnic identities, gender identities, sexual orientations, and socioeconomic backgrounds. For example, assessment, case studies, and practice problems highlight examples with inclusive gender categories, examples with women and gender nonbinary folks, samples with incarcerated women, Black and Latine youth, students from economically disadvantaged families, LGBTQ+ couples, and outcomes of community-based organization programming. Students learn how to apply statistical concepts to these case studies, while also critically evaluating how data and results can advocate for these populations. Thus, a key learning objective is understanding how to apply statistical concepts to social work settings with diverse clients and communities." />
<meta name="pb-primary-subject" content="JHBC" />
<meta name="pb-additional-subjects" content="J, JKS, JKSN" />
