<div class="front-matter acknowledgements " id="front-matter-acknowledgments" title="Acknowledgments">
	<div class="front-matter-title-wrap">
		<p class="front-matter-number">1</p>
		<h1 class="front-matter-title">Acknowledgments</h1>
								</div>
	<div class="ugc front-matter-ugc">
				 <p data-path-to-node="3,1">This book would not have been possible without the support of an Illinois State Library Grant. I am deeply grateful for this funding, which enabled the creation and publishing of this text. My sincere thanks go to the team of peer reviewers whose rigorous feedback and thoughtful critiques significantly sharpened the clarity of this text. I am also grateful to the editorial and publishing team for their guidance and expertise throughout the production process.</p> <p data-path-to-node="3,2">I want to express my profound gratitude to the countless students I have had the privilege of teaching in social work statistics over the last eight years. Your passion, curiosity, and commitment to social work energize me every single semester and give me hope for the future. You are the reason I teach and write, and I know you will change the world for the better.</p> <p data-path-to-node="3,4">I also extend my deepest appreciation to the incredible team of research assistants and student members of the Violence Prevention Research Lab at the School of Social Work. Because of your dedication and capacity to manage our ongoing projects, our research did not lose any momentum while my focus was split by the writing of this book. I am incredibly proud of the work you continue to do.</p> <p data-path-to-node="3,5">While this book is written for the future of social work, my own past, present, and future belong to the remarkable individuals who made me who I am.</p> <p data-path-to-node="3,6">First, to <b data-path-to-node="3,6" data-index-in-node="10">Dr. Kelley</b>, my very first statistics professor. It is impossible to fully measure the impact you have had on my career and the educator I have become. By introducing me to the pioneering work of Florence Nightingale, you showed me how data can be used to inform public health, transform social services, and advocate for the greater good. You taught me that statistics has a heart, and you showed me how to pass that passion on by making teaching fun. This book’s focus on applied, real-world statistics is a direct reflection of the legacy you handed down to me.</p> <p data-path-to-node="3,7">To <b data-path-to-node="3,7" data-index-in-node="3">Jack</b>, my husband and best friend. You have been by my side since my very first days of college, walking with me through every degree, countless late nights, and the entire journey to tenure. You are an incredible person—brilliant and deeply insightful. Thank you for always challenging me to be a better, more thoughtful person, and for providing the unwavering love and partnership that made this book, and everything else, possible.</p> <p data-path-to-node="3,8">Finally, to my beautiful boys, <b data-path-to-node="3,8" data-index-in-node="31">Jackson and Deacon</b>. Becoming your mom has been transformative, fundamentally shifting my perspective on the world. You are not just my daily inspiration and greatest joy—you are my everything.</p> 
	</div>
			
				
				
	</div>
<div class="part-wrapper" id="part-unit-i-introduction-to-statistics-wrapper">
    <div class="part  introduction" id="part-unit-i-introduction-to-statistics">
	<div class="part-title-wrap">
		<p class="part-number">I</p>
		<h1 class="part-title">Unit 1. Introduction to Statistics</h1>
	</div>
	<div class="ugc part-ugc">
		<p>We will start with an introduction to the utility of statistics in social work, highlight key concepts in research methods, discuss how we measure our variables, and end with how we can visualize our data.</p>
	</div>
			
				
	</div>
<div class="chapter standard " id="chapter-chapter-1-statistics-in-social-work" title="Chapter 1: Statistics in Social Work">
	<div class="chapter-title-wrap">
		<p class="chapter-number">1</p>
		<h1 class="chapter-title">Chapter 1: Statistics in Social Work</h1>
								</div>
	<div class="ugc chapter-ugc">
				 <div class="chapter-1:-statistics-in-social-work"><h2 class="import-Normal">The Social Work Profession</h2> <p class="import-Normal">The National Association of Social Workers (NASW) describes social workers as committed to “advocacy, social justice, and helping individuals, families, and communities who need us most” (NASW, 2025, para. 6). Social work encompasses an array of professions:</p> <ul><li class="import-Normal">Working to help individuals who are facing mental health concerns</li> <li class="import-Normal">Connecting people and families with resources and support</li> <li class="import-Normal">Providing counseling or therapy</li> <li class="import-Normal">Advocating for social justice</li> <li class="import-Normal">Providing crisis intervention or victim services</li> <li class="import-Normal">Strengthening community action by empowering residents</li> <li class="import-Normal">Working in spaces of rehabilitation</li> <li class="import-Normal">Engaging with families who are navigating the child welfare system</li> <li class="import-Normal">Challenging oppressive practices and policies</li> <li class="import-Normal">Evaluating clinical practice, programs, and interventions</li> </ul> <p class="import-Normal">Social workers are found across settings: community mental health centers, private practices, nonprofits, community-based organizations, social services departments, coalitions, state and federal agencies, hospitals and health care offices, early education facilities, schools, places of worship and spirituality, prisons, jails, probation/parole offices, police stations, military institutions, substance use centers, homeless shelters, domestic violence shelters, legal aid offices, research centers, colleges and universities, rehabilitation centers, corporations, state and federal legislatures, and so forth. As you can see from these professions and settings examples, social work as an occupation is incredibly diverse, and social workers are necessary in most settings (for additional information on the social work profession, see Segal et al. [2019]; for examples of social work practice across settings, see Grobman [2019]).</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" id="attachment_29" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-29" style="width: 700px"><img class="wp-image-25" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001-300x200.png" alt="A social worker sits opposite a client in a professional office." width="700" height="467" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001-300x200.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001-768x512.png 768w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001-65x43.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001-225x150.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001-350x233.png 350w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/001.png 810w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text" id="caption-attachment-29">Figure 1.1. “<a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/man-and-woman-sitting-on-the-couch-7345463/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/photo/man-and-woman-sitting-on-the-couch-7345463/">Man and Woman Sitting on the Couch</a>” by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/@shkrabaanthony/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/@shkrabaanthony/">Antoni Shkraba Studio</a>, via <a href="https://www.pexels.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/">Pexels</a>, 2021. <a href="https://www.pexels.com/license/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/license/">Pexels License</a>.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">Social workers contribute to the following types of social work:</p> <ul><li><strong><em>Micro social work</em></strong> (individual): direct practice with individual clients, families, and small groups of clients <ul><li>You may engage in individual case management, provide therapy to an individual with mental health concerns, engage in group counseling, provide crisis intervention, and so forth.</li> </ul> </li> <li><strong><em>M</em></strong><strong><em>ezzo social work</em></strong> (groups/organizations): practice with schools, families, communities, or teams <ul><li>You may develop and deliver group programs, workshops, or trainings; coordinate services across community-based organizations for an individual; and so forth. You are often found in the “middle” or “mezzo” between individuals and the groups and organizations they engage with.</li> </ul> </li> <li><strong><em>M</em></strong><strong><em>acro social work</em></strong> (systems/policy): practice with larger governing systems and policies, engaging in policy advocacy or administration work <ul><li>You may conduct a policy analysis, conduct research, help with community organizing events, or lead or direct community-based organizations or social service agencies.</li> </ul> </li> </ul> <p class="import-Normal">You will likely work across micro, mezzo, and macro social work because “social problems require complex and sustained intervention at all levels of social work practice” (Rothman &amp; Mizrahi, 2014, p. 91). For example, the American Academy of Social Work and Social Welfare published the <em>Grand Challenges for Social Work</em> (Barth &amp; Sherraden, 2018; Barth et al., 2022), highlighting key areas of social problems that require attention and action. Across these 13 grand challenges, it is clear how social work needs to see micro-mezzo-macro social work from an infused approach because these social problems are complex and cannot be solved by one level of intervention (e.g., Bent-Goodley et al., 2019). This push for an integrated approach is also in alignment with the profession’s “person-in-environment” perspective, which aims to study how one’s environment shapes their beliefs, behaviors, and perspectives. For more on this topic, please see Biscontini (2025) for an overview of person-in-environment in social work, or see the foundational work by Karls and Wandrei (1994).</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-29" style="width: 594px"><img class="wp-image-26 size-full" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/002.png" alt="Advocates holding signs and raising arms in the air." width="594" height="396" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/002.png 594w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/002-300x200.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/002-65x43.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/002-225x150.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/002-350x233.png 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 594px) 100vw, 594px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text">Figure 1.2. “<a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/crowd-of-protesters-holding-signs-4614165/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/photo/crowd-of-protesters-holding-signs-4614165/">Crowd of Protesters Holding Signs</a>” by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/@life-matters/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/@life-matters/">Life Matters</a>, via <a href="https://www.pexels.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/">Pexels</a>, 2020. <a href="https://www.pexels.com/license/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/license/">Pexels license</a>.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">No matter what area of social work you end up in, you will be learning various techniques and methodologies to make you an effective social worker. One area that you will be learning about is how research is fused into almost every aspect of social work.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">The Role of Research in Social Work</h2> <p class="import-Normal">As with any helping profession (e.g., psychology, medicine, education, public health), research is a critical component. Across codes of ethics guiding a profession, a common ethical principle is to “do no harm” and to prioritize the well-being, safety, and health of the people and communities served. For example, in the United States, ethical principles guide many professions (e.g., NASW, American Psychological Association [APA], National Institutes of Health [NIH], American Public Health Association [APHA], American Medical Association [AMA], National Education Association, etc.).</p> <p class="import-Normal"><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Research</span></em></strong> is a systematic method of inquiring, exploring, examining, or investigating questions, with the aim of discovering new findings, demonstrating impact, advocating for change, solving problems, or advancing theories. There are various methods to test and answer research questions, including collecting and organizing information and analyzing data. In social work, we often use applied research, which is research that aims to solve practical, real-world problems, while also strategizing how we can use research findings in practice.</p> <p class="import-Normal">So, across the helping professions, we can use research to ensure we do no harm. Research can help us learn what practices work; what does not work; and what needs to be modified, updated, or adapted. From research, we can ensure we are achieving our intended goals and outcomes, and make sure our clinical practices, interventions or policies are not harming participants and communities.</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-29" style="width: 446px"><img class="wp-image-27 size-full" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/003.png" alt="Group therapy session." width="446" height="297" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/003.png 446w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/003-300x200.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/003-65x43.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/003-225x150.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/003-350x233.png 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 446px) 100vw, 446px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text">Figure 1.3. “<a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/woman-discussing-problem-during-group-therapy-7176305/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/photo/woman-discussing-problem-during-group-therapy-7176305/">Woman discussing problem during group therapy</a>” by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/@shvets-production/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/@shvets-production/">SHVETS production</a>, via <a href="https://www.pexels.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/">Pexels</a>, 2021. <a href="https://www.pexels.com/license/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/license/">Pexels license</a>.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">In social services, we engage in <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">evidence-based practices</span></em></strong>: integrating the best research evidence, clinical expertise, and participant and community values into our work (Bent-Goodley et al., 2019). Social workers rely on research to inform them what interventions have demonstrated impact and use their clinical expertise to strategize modifications or adaptations. They also consider the values of their participants or communities to ensure that the interventions are relevant, meaningful, and aligned.</p> <p class="import-Normal">Engaging in evidence-based practices is the core of what social work is. Using research–whether you are collecting your own information and testing your own questions or you are reading about what other individuals researched (e.g., reading academic journal articles, evaluation briefs)–informs the decisions we make. We use evidence-based practices across the continuum of micro, mezzo, and macro social work: selecting our therapeutic modalities (e.g., cognitive-behavioral, psychodynamic, interpersonal process group therapy), our curricula (e.g., evidence-based prevention program), or our engagement practices (e.g., community organizing, policy advocacy, program evaluation). Thus, effective social workers use evidence-based information to make decisions.</p> <h3 class="import-Normal">An Example of Why Research Is Important</h3> <p class="import-Normal">To illustrate the importance of research, let’s consider evidence from the Drug Abuse Resistance Education (D.A.R.E.) program. D.A.R.E. provided a classroom lesson from a uniformed police officer on the dangers of drug use. This popular program ran from 1983 to 2009 in the United States. For example, during the 1990s, it was estimated that D.A.R.E. received $600-$750 million from the U.S. government (Berry, 2024).</p> <p class="import-Normal">However, research has consistently shown that D.A.R.E. does not work. In a study that reviewed research studies of D.A.R.E.’s impact, the authors found D.A.R.E. to be ineffective in preventing alcohol and drug use among youth in school (West &amp; O’Neal, 2004). Another study found that participants of D.A.R.E. became more likely to try drugs than kids not in the program (Rosenbaum &amp; Hanson, 1998). D.A.R.E. was created during the 1980’s War on Drugs and “Just Say No” campaigns; the program was created in a time of fear, and it was not created by prevention practitioners or researchers (Berry, 2024).</p> <p class="import-Normal">A new D.A.R.E. program has since emerged, called “keepin’ it REAL,” and has shown a reduction in student drug use (e.g., Kulis et al., 2007). The new program was developed by a nonprofit organization that included specialists in areas of substance use and prevention. The program also has an advisory board to provide oversight and review research. The curriculum is now multiple lessons with a mixture of videos, discussions, role-playing activities, and peer interactions. There is some research evidence to suggest the effectiveness of the new program in reducing drug use and shaping norms, attitudes, and resistance strategies (Hecht et al., 2003; Kulis et al., 2007). More research is still needed to evaluate the impact of this program, especially after the costliness and lack of effectiveness of the original D.A.R.E. program.</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-29" style="width: 544px"><img class="wp-image-28 size-full" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/004.png" alt="Empty school desks and chairs are lined up in rows in a classroom." width="544" height="364" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/004.png 544w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/004-300x201.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/004-65x43.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/004-225x151.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/004-350x234.png 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 544px) 100vw, 544px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text">Figure 1.4. “<a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/interior-of-abandoned-building-256395/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/photo/interior-of-abandoned-building-256395/">Interior of Abandoned Building</a>” by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/@pixabay/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/@pixabay/">Pixabay</a>, via <a href="https://www.pexels.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/">Pexels</a>, 2016.&nbsp;<a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">Public domain (CC0 1.0)</a>.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">Thus, conducting research and testing the practices and programs we implement are key to establishing and sustaining evidence-based practices in social work and all helping professions. As will be discussed in Chapter 2, there are different types of research, involving data that can include numbers or words. The focus of this book is on numerical data and how we can use these data to inform our research and social work practices.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal"><strong>Social Work Statistics</strong></h2> <p class="import-Normal"><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Statistical analyses</span></em></strong> are mathematical techniques used to organize, summarize, and interpret numerical data obtained from assessments, reports, research studies, and evaluations. Statistical analyses allow social workers to advocate for their participants, clients, patients, and communities; conduct their own research; evaluate their clinical practice; assess the impact of community programs; understand and learn from other people’s research; read and write reports; inform funding proposals; and make informed decisions about organizational operations.</p> <p class="import-Normal">Across statistical analyses, we often strive to understand a <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">population</span></em></strong>, or an entire set of individuals whom we are interested in learning about. It may not be feasible to collect data from an entire population, so we often rely on data from a <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">sample</span></em></strong>, or a subset of individuals from a given population. In social work, we conduct <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">needs assessments</span></em></strong>, which gather or collect data to understand the need for services and support for a population or sample in a service region or setting.</p> <h3 class="import-Normal">Example: Needs Assessment to Illustrate Students’ Mental Health</h3> <p class="import-Normal">A school social worker may conduct a needs assessment to illustrate the mental health needs of students in a school district. Ideally, it would be best to assess the mental health (e.g., depressive symptoms, suicidal ideation, anxiety, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, trauma symptoms, etc.) of every student in their school district. The social worker could then use these assessment data to plan for more universal programming to promote wellness among all students, provide more targeted therapeutic services for those who have elevated symptoms, and make informed community-based partner referrals as needed. Also, if the data indicated that a larger proportion of students needed therapeutic or community-based services, the social worker could use these data to advocate for more funding for another social worker position. Thus, obtaining needs assessment data from a population can be incredibly informative. In this example, if a social worker obtains data from <em>every</em> student in the school district, this would be population data. The numerical values used to summarize data from a population are called <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">parameters</span></em></strong>.</p> <p class="import-Normal">However, it is usually not feasible to collect data from an entire population. Thus, we often must rely on obtaining data from a <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">sample</span></em></strong>, or a subset of individuals from a given population (see Figure 1.5).</p> <p class="import-Normal">If the school social worker is in a district that has 1,000 total students in 1st to 12th grade, they may sample 300 students. Because they still want to understand the larger population the students come from, they will strive to collect a representative sample (e.g., 25 students from each grade level). Obtaining representative samples is important. Imagine if the social worker collected a sample of 300 students, but they only had 1st- and 2nd-grade students in their sample; this would not be reflective of the entire student body if they did not capture the experiences of students in grades 3–12 in their sample. The numerical values used to summarize data from a sample are called <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">statistics</span></em></strong>.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-29" style="width: 888px"><img class="wp-image-29" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-1024x481.png" alt="A large circle labeled &amp;quot;population&amp;quot; includes lots of smaller circles inside labeled &amp;quot;student.&amp;quot; This circle is labeled &amp;quot;Population: all students in the district.&amp;quot; Next to this circle is an arrow and a smaller circle labeled &amp;quot;Sample: subset of students from the district&amp;quot; to show that this sample came from the population. This smaller circle also has smaller circles inside it labeled &amp;quot;students.&amp;quot;" width="888" height="417" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-1024x481.png 1024w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-300x141.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-768x361.png 768w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-1536x721.png 1536w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-65x31.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-225x106.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005-350x164.png 350w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/005.png 1908w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 888px) 100vw, 888px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text">Figure 1.5. A sample is a subset of a population.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">This book includes examples of population parameters and sample statistics. A larger emphasis is placed on sample statistics, given that we rely on samples in most of our research and practice. Additionally, as you will learn in later units of this book, we can conduct an array of statistical tests that allow us to effectively use samples to make conclusions about the populations from which they are drawn.</p> <h2>Overarching Types of Analyses</h2> <p class="import-Normal">Finally, there are two overarching types of analyses you will learn about in this book:</p> <ul><li><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Descriptive analyses</span></em></strong>: Descriptive parameters and statistics are used to organize, summarize, and communicate information about your data. The goal of descriptive analyses is to reduce a large amount of information into smaller “bullet points.” For example, in the school social worker example, they may find that 20% of all students have clinically significant levels of anxiety. The parameter is 20%, and it describes the prevalence of anxiety in the school. If the social worker finds that 10% of the sample of 300 students has clinically significant levels of depression, the statistic is 10%, and it describes the prevalence of depression.</li> <li><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Inferential analyses</span></em></strong>: Inferential statistics use samples to draw conclusions about a population using specific tests. Thus, we use inferential statistics with samples; you will learn about an array of inferential tests later in this book. In the school social worker example, they may think that students’ use of social media is contributing to their heightened negative mental health symptoms. In their sampling of 300 students, they may also assess students’ social media use and then test their hypothesis. If their sample shows that there is a significant association between social media use and anxiety symptoms (e.g., higher levels of social media use are related to higher levels of anxiety symptoms), they can infer with a certain degree of confidence that this association would also exist if they tested this question with the full 1,000 students (at the population level).</li> </ul> <h2 class="import-Normal">Summary</h2> <p class="import-Normal">The social work profession is diverse; however, across all settings and activities, social workers use evidence-based practices. This book will guide you through the various ways in which statistical analyses are used in social work, informing and advancing our field. We will focus on descriptive parameters and statistics first, looking at the ways in which we can describe a population and a sample. We will then examine different inferential statistics, which we can use to test research questions with samples. This book will use social work examples so that you can see the applied, direct applications of statistics in our profession. Statistics can be used as a valuable tool to help us ensure we are doing no harm and that we are engaging in (and contributing to) evidence-based practice.</p> <p class="import-Normal">This book is written to be more of a practical guide to statistics, with overarching definitions and examples of applying these approaches in social work practice. This book is written with a strong emphasis on practical applications, with theoretical components as more of a side note.</p> </div> 
	</div>
			
				
				
	</div>
<div class="chapter standard " id="chapter-chapter-2-an-introduction-to-research-methods" title="Chapter 2: An Introduction to Research Methods">
	<div class="chapter-title-wrap">
		<p class="chapter-number">2</p>
		<h1 class="chapter-title">Chapter 2: An Introduction to Research Methods</h1>
								</div>
	<div class="ugc chapter-ugc">
				 <div class="chapter-2:-an-introduction-to-research-methods"><p class="import-Normal">In Chapter 1, we read about the importance of research in the helping professions and its role in being an evidence-based social worker. This chapter will provide an overview of introductory research methods, particularly concepts that are linked to the statistics we will be learning in this book.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Scientific Method</h2> <p class="import-Normal">The <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">scientific method</span></em></strong> is the process by which we test a research question, consisting of seven steps, as shown in Figure 2.1:&nbsp;including (1) ask a question, (2) conduct background research, (3) generate testable hypothesis, (4) design the study, (5) gather data to test hypothesis, (6) analyze results, and (7) draw conclusions.</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" id="attachment_33" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-33" style="width: 700px"><img class="wp-image-32" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-1024x709.png" alt="The seven steps of the scientific method are shown as a cycle." width="700" height="484" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-1024x709.png 1024w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-300x208.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-768x532.png 768w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-1536x1063.png 1536w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-65x45.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-225x156.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006-350x242.png 350w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/006.png 1624w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text" id="caption-attachment-33">Figure 2.1. The scientific method.</div></div> <ul><li><strong>Ask a question</strong>: In social work, you may be interested in asking applied research questions, such as “Was this intervention effective?” or “Did my client see significant changes in their anxiety symptoms from participating in group therapy?”</li> <li><strong>Conduct background research</strong>: This is where you will conduct research on what others have found in similar areas of work. You may go to a database or review Google Scholar to see what other researchers have theorized and found in their own studies. For example, if you are testing whether an intervention is effective, then you may want to review other studies that tested the impact of this intervention. Did they find that the intervention was effective? Was the sample similar to your participants?</li> <li><strong>Generate testable hypothes</strong><strong>i</strong><strong>s:</strong> Based on the research you just reviewed in Step 2, if there is enough evidence to inform you of a hypothesis, you may generate your own prediction of what will happen when you test your research question with your own sample. For example, if previous research found that the intervention you are implementing was effective, you may also hypothesize that the intervention will be effective with your sample of participants.</li> <li><strong>Design the study</strong>: Next, you get to design your study. There is an array of design choices to make here (which will be covered more in depth in research methods courses). A common design in social work research, especially when testing an intervention, is to give your participants two assessments: one assessment before they start the intervention to establish the baseline levels of your outcome and one assessment after they complete the intervention. This design will let you see if there are meaningful changes in the intended outcome of the intervention from baseline to follow-up.</li> <li><strong>Gather data to test hypothesis</strong>: This step is where you will collect data to test your research question and hypothesis. If you are using numerical data to test your question, you may collect self-report survey assessments from your participants.</li> <li><strong>Analyze results</strong>: Once you have gathered data, you will then use descriptive and inferential statistics to see if your hypothesis was correct. Throughout this book, you will learn some of the analytic methods to test hypotheses.</li> <li><strong>Draw conclusions</strong>: From your data analysis, you will draw conclusions. If you see a significant change in your intended outcome from baseline to follow-up, you may conclude that your intervention is showing effectiveness. If you do not, you may generate more research questions to understand why it was not effective. In this book, we will examine how you can write up conclusions based on the design and data analysis that you selected.</li> </ul> <h3 class="import-Normal" style="margin-left: 18pt;">Example: Applying the Scientific Method</h3> <p class="import-Normal" style="margin-left: 18pt;">Your school district is implementing a social-emotional learning program with its middle school students. Social-emotional skills, such as understanding and regulating one’s emotions, building and practicing empathy, building healthy friendships and peer relationships, making responsible decisions, and setting goals, are all key skills to develop during childhood (McClelland et al., 2017; Zins et al., 2007). The social workers at the school are asked to help research the effectiveness of the program because the school administrators want to know if they should keep funding it. The scientific method can be used test this research question: “Is the social-emotional learning program effective at improving social-emotional skills among students who participate in the program?”</p> <p class="import-Normal" style="margin-left: 18pt;">Using the seven steps of the scientific method, we can test this research question:</p> <ul><li><strong>Ask a question:</strong> Is the social-emotional learning program effective at improving social-emotional skills among students who participate in the program?</li> <li><strong>Conduct background research</strong>: You use library databases to research what previous studies have found. In your research, you find that other middle school students reported improvements to their emotion regulation, social skills, sense of responsibility, and decision making.</li> <li><strong>Generate testable hypothesis</strong>: Based on this research, you hypothesize that if students participate in the school’s program, they will show improvements in their social-emotional skills.</li> <li><strong>Design the study</strong>: To test your hypothesis, you will assess levels of social-emotional skills at baseline and again after students participate in the program.</li> <li><strong>Gather data to test hypothesis:</strong> You collect self-report surveys from students and teacher-report surveys about their students at two time points (before and after program participation).</li> <li><strong>Analyze results</strong>: You conduct a dependent samples t-test (see Chapter 16) to analyze these data and see if there is a significant difference in levels of social-emotional skills before and after the program among students who participated in the program.</li> <li><strong>Draw conclusions</strong>: After you analyze the data, you find that there are significant improvements in levels of social emotional skills among students who participated in the program. You conclude that this program is showing promising results, and thus the school should keep funding the program.</li> </ul> <p class="import-Normal">The scientific method is the foundation for research methods; it can be used to help us answer questions, generate new hypotheses, test different design and data analysis choices, and strengthen our conclusions.</p> <h3 class="import-Normal">Research From the Scientific Method: A Core Component of Evidence-Based Practice</h3> <p class="import-Normal">In social work, we utilize the scientific method, or research that uses the scientific method, to inform evidence-based practices (EBPs). As we read in Chapter 1, EBPs are a process in which social workers make practice decisions based on the best research evidence available, their experience and expertise, and the needs and values of their participant or community (see Figure 2.2).</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-33" style="width: 849px"><img class="wp-image-33" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-1024x683.png" alt="The three components of evidence-based practice are shown: best research evidence, social worker experience and expertise, and participant and community values and needs." width="849" height="566" srcset="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-1024x683.png 1024w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-300x200.png 300w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-768x512.png 768w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-1536x1024.png 1536w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-65x43.png 65w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-225x150.png 225w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007-350x233.png 350w, https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/007.png 1588w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 849px) 100vw, 849px" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text">Figure 2.2. Evidence-based practice.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">In the United States and its territories, social work baccalaureate and master’s programs are guided by the Council on Social Work Education (CSWE), which provides accreditation standards for educational institutions. Education for social workers promotes EBP through “scientific inquiry.” The CSWE (2022) highlights nine competencies for social workers, including several that elevate the critical role of EBPs. For example, Competency 4 (Engage in Practice-Informed Research and Research-Informed Practice) states the following:</p> <p class="import-Normal" style="margin-left: 36pt;">Social workers use ethical, culturally informed, anti-racist, and anti-oppressive approaches in conducting research and building knowledge. Social workers use research to inform their practice decision making and articulate how their practice experience informs research and evaluation decisions. Social workers critically evaluate and critique current, empirically sound research to inform decisions pertaining to practice, policy, and programs. Social workers understand the inherent bias in research and evaluate design, analysis, and interpretation using an anti-racist and anti-oppressive perspective. Social workers know how to access, critique, and synthesize the current literature to develop appropriate research questions and hypotheses. Social workers demonstrate knowledge and skills regarding qualitative and quantitative research methods and analysis, and they interpret data derived from these methods. Social workers demonstrate knowledge about methods to assess reliability and validity in social work research. Social workers can articulate and share research findings in ways that are usable to a variety of clients and constituencies. Social workers understand the value of evidence derived from interprofessional and diverse research methods, approaches, and sources. (CSWE, 2022, p. 10)</p> <p>Other competencies also discuss the importance of EBPs in social work, including Competency 1 (Demonstrate Ethical and Professional Behavior) which calls for ethical conduct of research, and Competency 8 (Intervene with Individuals, Families, Groups, Organizations, and Communities), which states, “social workers understand methods of identifying, analyzing, and implementing evidence-informed interventions” (CSWE, 2022, p. 12).</p> <p class="import-Normal">So, even if a social worker does not directly use the scientific method to test a research question or is not the person running data analyses, they are still using research to inform their practices (e.g., to shape which therapeutic modalities to use, to inform which programs should be invested in or which policies should be modified). Understanding some of the basics of research methods will allow you to ask and answer a research question or to read research and understand what took place to inform the researcher’s conclusions.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Quantitative and Qualitative Methods</h2> <p class="import-Normal">In research, two methods are used: quantitative and qualitative methods. <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Quantitative methods </span> </em></strong>use numerical data to examine differences and relationships. You will be learning some of the statistical tests to answer these types of research questions later in this book. For example, when we examine differences, we might want to test if there is a difference in average levels of a certain type of behavior between two groups. We may also be interested in differences in an attitude or behavior from before to after an intervention. When we examine relationships, we may be interested in hypotheses that examine whether an increase in one variable predicts increases or decreases in another variable. Across these different types of tests, these numbers can be obtained from publicly available datasets, collected from surveys or interviews, and counted or tallied during observations. Statistical analyses use quantitative data.</p> <p class="import-Normal"><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Qualitative methods</span></em></strong> often use non-numerical data, such as text or audio from interviews, focus groups, or observations, that explore and examine themes and experiences. Some researchers use both quantitative and qualitative methods (i.e., mixed methods), where they try to find deeper meaning from both numerical and non-numerical data. See Rubin and Babbie (2024) for more discussion on quantitative, qualitative, and mixed-methods research in social work.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Variables</h2> <p class="import-Normal">When a research question is asked, we need to dissect the components of the hypothesis. First, we need to identify the variables within the hypothesis. <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Variables</span></em></strong> are any observation of a physical attribute, attitude, behavior, characteristic, mood, experience, and so forth that can take on different values. Variables have more than one value, whereas a constant is an attribute with only one value.</p> <p class="import-Normal">Let’s go back to the example of the social-emotional learning program described earlier in this chapter. Our hypothesis was as follows:</p> <p class="import-Normal" style="margin-left: 36pt;">If students <strong>participate in the school’s program</strong>, they will show improvements in their <strong>social-emotional skills.</strong></p> <p class="import-Normal">In this question, we have two variables that we are going examine (also bolded in the hypothesis):</p> <ul><li><strong>Participation in the program</strong>: This is a variable because students either participate in the program (yes) or do not (no).</li> <li><strong>Social emotional skills</strong>: This is a variable because students may have a range of social-emotional skills (from poor to strong skill levels).</li> </ul> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-33" style="width: 600px"><img class="wp-image-63" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2026/07/008-scaled-1.jpg" alt="" width="600" height="401" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text">Figure 2.3. Young children are sitting in chairs at desks in a school classroom. A teacher is next to one of the desks assisting a student. “<a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/children-sitting-on-brown-chairs-inside-the-classroom-4019754/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/photo/children-sitting-on-brown-chairs-inside-the-classroom-4019754/">Children Sitting on Brown Chairs Inside the Classroom</a>”&nbsp;by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/@artstel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/@artstel/">Arthur Krijgsman</a>, via <a href="https://www.pexels.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/">Pexels</a>, 2015. <a href="https://www.pexels.com/license/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/license/">Pexels license</a>.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">For both of our variables, we are going to use quantitative methods to collect numerical data. For any hypothesis, we want to pull out the number of variables we are examining because this will guide what type of statistical test we are going to conduct. In this book, we will be focusing on research questions with one or two variables:</p> <ul><li><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Univariate statistics</span></em></strong>: analyses that summarize numerical information about a single variable.</li> <li><strong><em><span class="glossary-term">Bivariate statistics</span>:</em></strong> analyses that examine differences and relationships between two variables.</li> </ul> <p>In our example, we have two variables (participation in the program and social emotional skills), so we will look at bivariate statistical analyses to test our research question. More advanced statistical methods may use <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">multivariate statistics</span></em></strong>, or analyses that examine differences and relationships between three or more variables. If we wanted to also look at the impact of participation in the program and the gender of the participant on social-emotional skills and anxiety symptoms, then we would use a multivariate statistical analysis because we would now have four variables to consider.</p> <p class="import-Normal">When you have bivariate (and multivariate) statistics, you also want to identify independent and dependent variables. As you’ll see throughout the chapters of this book that are focused on inferential tests, we must identify the independent and dependent variables so that we can select the appropriate statistical test.</p> <ul><li>An <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">independent variable</span></em></strong> is what causes change or affects the other variable (also known as a predictor variable). <ul><li>Across disciplines, you may see the independent variable also referred to as a <em>grouping variable</em> or <em>subject variable</em>. An independent variable is traditionally what the researcher changes, modifies, or manipulates for an experimental design (see Rubin and Babbie [2024] for more on experimental designs in social work). Thus, how these groups or conditions are organized may be referred to as your <em>grouping variable</em>. Additionally, you may see the term <em>subject variable</em> used to describe variables. These characteristics of your participants are preexisting (e.g., race, gender); however, these groups or categorizations can be examined as independent variables to see if they change or affect another variable.</li> </ul> </li> <li>A <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">dependent variable</span></em></strong> is what is affected or changed by the independent variable. The dependent variable is what is hypothesized to vary or change depending on the independent variable (also known as an outcome variable). <ul><li>Across disciplines, you may also see the dependent variable referred to as a <em>criterion variable</em>. Some researchers prefer the term <em>criterion variable</em> in research that does not involve experimental designs.</li> </ul> </li> </ul> <p class="import-Normal">A good way to practice identifying independent and dependent variables is to write your hypothesis using an “if-then” statement:</p> <p class="import-Normal" style="margin-left: 36pt;"><strong>If</strong> students <strong>participate in the school’s program</strong> (independent variable), <strong>then</strong> they will show improvements in their <strong>social-emotional skills</strong> (dependent variable).</p> <p class="import-Normal">So, the independent variable is the “if <em>x</em>….” part of the statement, and the dependent variable is the “then <em>y</em>…” part. In other words, your dependent variable <em>depends</em> on your independent variable. In this example, social-emotional skills <em>depend</em> on student participation in the program.</p> <p class="import-Normal">Sometimes our research questions have multiple independent variables or multiple dependent variables (in multivariate statistics). You may want to see which independent variable is the most influential on a dependent variable. However, in this book, we will focus on univariate parameters, univariate statistics, and bivariate statistics.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Summary</h2> <p class="import-Normal">This chapter highlighted some of the core concepts of introductory research methods, including the scientific method as a framework for testing our research questions, how research from the scientific method is a core component of EBP, and some terminology for quantitative variables. Identifying your independent and dependent variables is critical for selecting appropriate statistical tests, as you will see demonstrated later in this book.</p> </div> 
	</div>
			
				
				
	</div>
<div class="chapter standard " id="chapter-chapter-3-measurement" title="Chapter 3: Measurement">
	<div class="chapter-title-wrap">
		<p class="chapter-number">3</p>
		<h1 class="chapter-title">Chapter 3: Measurement</h1>
								</div>
	<div class="ugc chapter-ugc">
				 <div class="chapter-3:-measurement"><p class="import-Normal">In the previous chapter, we covered the scientific method and how to identify information about your quantitative variables from your hypothesis. We learned about identifying how many variables you have and identifying your independent and dependent variables in bivariate statistics. This chapter will focus on another aspect of your quantitative variables: how we measure them.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Measuring Our Variables Numerically</h2> <p class="import-Normal">How we measure our constructs or variables is called <strong><em><span class="glossary-term">operationalization</span></em></strong>, or writing an operational definition. This process translates a variable into measurable terms. As we make our variables measurable, we get to start making decisions about how we want to gather these data, too. For example, do we want to ask participants questions directly? Do we want to count things we observe? Do we want to have participants answer questions on their own?</p> <p class="import-Normal">As you continue in your social work education, you will be learning more about assessments in social work (e.g., Corcoran &amp; Walsh, 2022; Walker &amp; Beckett, 2011), including assessments using both quantitative and qualitative methods (Jordan &amp; Franklin, 2020; Rubin &amp; Babbie, 2024). Assessment is the process of gathering information about your participant and their presenting situation. The assessment process is guided by the person-in-environment perspective (as introduced in Chapter 1), which involves social workers gathering information about the individual and their environment (e.g., relationships, community, societal factors).</p> <p class="import-Normal">In this book, we will focus on numerical methods for measuring our variables. Remember, you may also collect qualitative assessments, although this is not the focus of this book.</p> <h3 class="import-Normal">Example: Measuring Social Support and Anxiety Symptoms</h3> <p class="import-Normal">In this chapter, we’ll examine the following research question and hypothesis:</p> <ul><li><strong>Research Question:</strong> Does having higher levels of social support decrease symptoms of anxiety?</li> <li><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> <strong>If</strong> my clients have higher levels of <strong>social support</strong>, <strong>then</strong> they will see reductions in <strong>anxiety symptoms</strong>. (Note that the independent variable is social support, and the dependent variable is symptoms of anxiety.)</li> </ul> <p class="import-Normal">To collect data and test this hypothesis with bivariate statistics, we must operationalize (measure) our two variables. For our independent variable, social support, we find a self-report questionnaire called the Multidimensional Scale of Perceived Social Support (Zimet et al., 1998). This questionnaire has 12 questions, rated on a scale of 1 (very strongly disagree) to 7 (very strongly agree). See Table 3.1.</p> <table style="height: 803px;"><caption>Table 3.1. Items and Responses From the Multidimensional Scale of Perceived Social Support (Zimet et al., 1998)</caption> <thead><tr style="height: 53px;"><th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: bottom; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 391px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Items</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 128px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Very Strongly Disagree</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 104px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Strongly Disagree</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 91px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Mildly Disagree</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 55.0312px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Neutral</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 76px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Mildly Agree</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 89px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Strongly Agree</strong></p> </th> <th class="shaded" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 113px;" scope="colgroup"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Very Strongly Agree</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 70px;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 391px;">There is a special person who is around when I am in need.</td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 128px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 104px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 91px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">3</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 55.0312px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 76px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">5</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 89px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">6</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 113px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">7</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 70px;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 391px;">There is a special person with whom I can share joys and sorrows.</td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 128px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 104px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 91px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">3</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 55.0312px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 76px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">5</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 89px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">6</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 113px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">7</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 52px;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 391px;">My family really tries to help me.</td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 128px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 104px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 91px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">3</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 55.0312px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 76px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">5</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 89px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">6</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 52px; width: 113px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">7</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 70px;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 391px;">I get the emotional help and support I need from my family.</td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 128px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 70px; width: 104px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C"