/p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">18.2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">9.6</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">10.4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">7.2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">7.8</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">14.4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">15.6</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.45pt;"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 100.344px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Difference <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=KE8g4oCTIEUp&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="(O – E)" title="(O – E)" class="latex mathjax" /></strong></p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">0.2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">–0.2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1.4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">–1.4</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">–2.2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">2.2</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">0.6</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">–0.6</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 28.25pt;"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 100.344px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Difference <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=KE8g4oCTIEUpXjI&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="(O – E)^2" title="(O – E)^2" class="latex mathjax" /></strong></p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">0.04</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">0.04</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1.96</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">1.96</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">4.84</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">4.84</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">0.36</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">0.36</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 35.5pt;"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 100.344px;" scope="row"><strong><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7KE8gLSBFKV4yfXtFfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}" title="\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}" class="latex mathjax" /></strong></th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7LjA0fXsxNi44fSA9IC4wMDI&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{.04}{16.8} = .002" title="\frac{.04}{16.8} = .002" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7LjA0fXsxOC4yfSA9IC4wMDI&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{.04}{18.2} = .002" title="\frac{.04}{18.2} = .002" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7MS45Nn17OS42fSA9IC4yMA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{1.96}{9.6} = .20" title="\frac{1.96}{9.6} = .20" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7MS45Nn17MTAuNH0gPSAuMTk&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{1.96}{10.4} = .19" title="\frac{1.96}{10.4} = .19" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7NC44NH17Ny4yfSA9IC42Nw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{4.84}{7.2} = .67" title="\frac{4.84}{7.2} = .67" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 120.078px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7NC44NH17Ny44fSA9IC42Mg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{4.84}{7.8} = .62" title="\frac{4.84}{7.8} = .62" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7LjM2fXsxNC40fSA9IC4wMw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{.36}{14.4} = .03" title="\frac{.36}{14.4} = .03" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 53px; width: 111.672px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGZyYWN7LjM2fXsxNS42fSA9IC4wMg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\frac{.36}{15.6} = .02" title="\frac{.36}{15.6} = .02" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p class="import-Normal"><em>Note. </em>AAA = Asian or Asian American; BAA = Black or African American.</p> <p class="import-Normal">Then, you solve for your chi-square obtained value.</p> <div class="textbox textbox--examples"><div class="textbox__header"><h4 class="textbox__title">Example 20.3. Calculating Chi-Square Obtained Value</h4> </div> <div class="textbox__content"><p><strong>Formula for <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGNoaV4y&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\chi^2" title="\chi^2" class="latex mathjax" />:</strong></p> <p><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGNoaV4yID0gXHN1bSBcbGVmdChcZnJhY3soTyAtIEUpXjJ9e0V9XHJpZ2h0KQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\chi^2 = \sum \left(\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}\right)" title="\chi^2 = \sum \left(\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}\right)" class="latex mathjax" /></p> <p><strong>Our example:&nbsp;</strong></p> <p><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGNoaV4yPSguMDAyKy4wMDIrLjIwKy4xOSsuNjcrLjYyKy4wMysuMDIpID0gMS43Mw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\chi^2=(.002+.002+.20+.19+.67+.62+.03+.02) = 1.73" title="\chi^2=(.002+.002+.20+.19+.67+.62+.03+.02) = 1.73" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </div> </div> <h3 class="import-Normal">Step 4: Make a Decision</h3> <p class="import-Normal">You will compare your obtained value (1.73) to your critical value (7.815). Your obtained value does <em>not</em> exceed the critical value, so you would <strong>f</strong><strong>ail to reject the null hypothesis. </strong>This means that your two nominal variables are independent; there is not an association between them.</p> <h3 class="import-Normal">APA-Style Write-up</h3> <p class="import-Normal">In your APA-style write-up, you will include your decision (significant or not significant difference), your degrees of freedom and sample size in parentheses, your obtained value, and your <em>p</em> statement:</p> <p class="import-Normal" style="padding-left: 40px;">There was not a significant association in preferences for these four therapeutic approaches among social workers by racial identity, χ<sup>2</sup>(3, <em>N</em> = 100) = 1.73, <em>p</em> &gt; .05.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Summary</h2> <p class="import-Normal">The chi-square tests presented in this chapter give you an idea of how you can examine univariate and bivariate nominal variables, including associations between two nominal variables using the chi-square test of independence. Chi-square tests are often reported when a cross-tabulation table is presented in reports or papers.</p> </div> 
	</div>
			
				
				
	</div>

</div>
<div class="part-wrapper" id="part-conclusion-wrapper">
    <div class="part  " id="part-conclusion">
	<div class="part-title-wrap">
		<p class="part-number">VII</p>
		<h1 class="part-title">Conclusion</h1>
	</div>
	<div class="ugc part-ugc">
		<div class="chapter-21:-conclusion"><p>This book provides you with an introduction to statistics, with a focus on applied statistics in social work practice. We reviewed how to calculate descriptive statistics and inferential statistics, both of which can provide evidence for program or intervention impact, inform treatment decisions, or inform policy. These mathematical techniques can be used to organize, summarize, and interpret data, whether you are collecting your own data, reviewing existing data, or reading about other people’s research.</p> <p class="import-Normal">As was stressed in Chapter 1, social workers rely on statistics to make important decisions. Even if you do not calculate statistics of your own in the future, it is essential that you are able to read about statistics and keep up to date on evidence-based practices and scientific literature. Being versed in statistics is in alignment with Council on Social Work Education (CSWE) Core competencies because these skills will make you a stronger advocate and social worker.</p> <div class="wp-caption aligncenter" id="attachment_345" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-345" style="width: 600px"><img class="wp-image-345" src="https://pb-sandbox.library.illinois.edu/swstats3/wp-content/uploads/sites/389/2025/10/091.png" alt="Advocate holding a sign, “fight today for a better tomorrow,” and megaphone." width="600" height="400" title="" /><div class="wp-caption-text" id="caption-attachment-345">Figure C.1. “<a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-woman-in-pink-shirt-holding-a-megaphone-8899255/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-woman-in-pink-shirt-holding-a-megaphone-8899255/">A Woman in a Pink Shirt Holding a Megaphone</a>” by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/@lara-jameson/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/@lara-jameson/">Lara Jameson</a>, via <a href="https://www.pexels.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/">Pexels</a>, 2021. <a href="https://www.pexels.com/license/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-url="https://www.pexels.com/license/">Pexels license</a>.</div></div> <p class="import-Normal">From this foundational knowledge of social work statistics, there are many ways to continue expanding your learning. You may take a course in research methods, where you learn different ways to design research studies, collect different types of data (with all different levels of measurement!), learn analytic approaches to qualitative research, or learn how to write and validate your own assessment tools. You may take additional courses in statistics, where you can look at multivariate statistics; there are so many different inferential statistics to learn about and expand your toolbox of how you can approach and test research questions.</p> <p class="import-Normal">There are also courses where you can learn computer software programs that calculate descriptive and inferential statistics for you. With the knowledge you gained in this book, you’ll be informed about where these numbers come from, how to interpret them, and how to write up results in American Psychological Association (APA)-style format. For example, the Data ToolPak in Excel is a free add-on, and it can calculate all of the statistics we learned in this book.</p> <p class="import-Normal">The remainder of this chapter includes summaries of key elements, including concepts, notation, symbols, and equations.</p> <h2 class="import-Normal">Unit 1: Introduction to Statistics</h2> <p>Tables C.1 and C.2 summarize key concepts, notations, symbols, and equations from Unit 1.</p> <table class="grid" style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.1. Unit 1 Concepts</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Concept</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Definition or Details</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Research</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Systematic method of inquiring, exploring, examining, or investigating questions, with the aim of discovering new findings, demonstrating impact, advocating for change, solving problems, or advancing theories</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Evidence-based practices</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Integrating the best research evidence, clinical expertise, and participant and community values</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Populations and parameters</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">A population is an entire set of individuals we are interested in learning about. Numerical values used to summarize data from a population are called <em>parameters</em>.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Samples and statistics</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">A sample is a subset of individuals from a given population. Numerical values used to summarize data from a sample are called <em>statistics</em>.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Descriptive analyses</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Descriptive parameters and statistics are used to organize, summarize, and communicate information about your data.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Inferential statistics</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Inferential statistics use samples to draw conclusions about a population using specific tests. Thus, we use inferential statistics with samples.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Scientific method</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">The process in which we test a research question: (1) Ask a question, (2) conduct background research, (3) generate testable hypotheses, (4) design the study, (5) gather data to test hypotheses, (6) analyze results, and (7) draw conclusions.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Quantitative methods</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Methods that use numerical data to examine relationships and differences</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Qualitative methods</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Methods that often use non-numerical data, such as text or audio from interviews, focus groups, or observations, to explore and examine themes and experiences</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Variables</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Any observation of a physical attribute, attitude, behavior, characteristic, mood, experience, and so forth, that can take on different values</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Univariate statistics</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Analyses that summarize numerical information about a single variable</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Bivariate statistics</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Analyses that examine differences and relationships between two variables</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Multivariate statistics</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Analyses that examine differences and relationships between three or more variables.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Independent variable</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">A variable that causes change or affects the other variable; also known as a <em>predictor variable</em></p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Dependent variable</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">A variable that is affected or changed by the independent variable. The dependent variable is what is hypothesized to vary or change depending on the independent variable. Also known as an <em>outcome variable.&nbsp;</em></p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Operationalization</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">The process of how we measure our variables; translating a variable into measurable terms</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Level of measurement</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Ways in which the values of a variable are expressed or scaled</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Nominal variables (categorical)</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Nominal variables are the lowest level of measurement; numerals are assigned to categorize (label) various characteristics or attributes, although these numerals are arbitrary and are simply used to organize the data.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Ordinal variables (categorical)</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Ordinal variables organize values with rank ordering (e.g., first, second, etc.). This allows us to classify or organize values again and allows us to put those categories in a particular order.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Interval variables (continuous)</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Interval variables classify a variable’s values into order but we also know the interval or distance between values. There is no true zero point, though.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Ratio variables (continuous)</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Ratio variables are the highest level of measurement; ratio variable values are used to classify, they go in a particular order, we know the interval between values, and there is a true zero point (i.e., zero represents the absence of what you are measuring).</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Distributions and Frequency Distributions</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;"><p class="import-Normal">Distributions show us how groups of values are organized, and frequency distributions group together cases with the same values. Tables are useful for displaying frequency distributions.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><th style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 172.125px;" rowspan="3" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal">Figures</p> </th> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;" scope="rowgroup"><p class="import-Normal">Figures are when charts, pictures, or graphs are used to help display and describe data.</p> </td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;" scope="rowgroup"><p class="import-Normal">Bar charts and pie or donut charts are common figures for categorical variables.</p> </td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 430.82px;" scope="rowgroup"><p class="import-Normal">Histograms and frequency polygons are common figures for continuous variables.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.2. Unit 1 Symbols and Equations</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Symbol or Equation</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Description</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=Tg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="N" title="N" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Total number of participants in a population or sample</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=bg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="n" title="n" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Subset of participants (e.g., subsample)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 29.65pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHRleHR7UGVyY2VudGFnZX0gPSBcZnJhY3tufXtOfSBcdGltZXMgMTAw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\text{Percentage} = \frac{n}{N} \times 100" title="\text{Percentage} = \frac{n}{N} \times 100" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Percentage calculation</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 class="import-Normal">Unit 2: Descriptive, Univariate Parameters and Statistics</h2> <p>Tables C.3–C.5 summarize key concepts, notations, symbols, and equations from Unit 2.</p> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.3. Unit 2 Concepts</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Concept</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Definition or Details</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Central tendency</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">A value (or values) that describe the most typical, average, middle, or common value in a data set</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Mode</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The value or values in a data set that are the most frequently occurring (the most common value). Can be used at all levels of measurement.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Median</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The value at the midpoint in an ordered data set (the middle, with the same number of values above and below it). Must have an ordinal level of measurement or higher.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Mean</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The value found by calculating the average of a data set <a id="_Hlk207188436" href="" data-url=""></a>(i.e., taking the sum of all values, then dividing by the number of cases or people in your population or sample). Must have an interval or ratio level of measurement.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Variability</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">A value that describes the dispersion or spread of values around a point of central tendency</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Range</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Distance separating the highest from the lowest values</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Sum of squares</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Squared deviation scores (which represent how far each value is from the mean) to measure overall variability</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Variance</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Another way to measure overall variability, but it accounts for the size of your population or sample, by dividing your sum of squares by <em>N</em></p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Standard deviation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Another way to measure overall variability, but it removes squared units (which variance is in squared units). You’ll take the square root of the variance to find the standard deviation.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Normal distribution</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Also referred to as the <em>bell-shaped curve</em> because of its shape, the normal distribution is a theoretical distribution. This means that the distribution resulted from mathematical principles rather than actual data. Thus, it provides a visual reference and the key features of how a variable is expected to look (i.e., be distributed). In statistics, we can use this theoretical model and compare our actual data distributions to it; we can approximate it.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Kurtosis</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Assesses the “peakedness” of a distribution, which reflects the weight of the distribution tails. A normal distribution has a mesokurtic shape. When a distribution has high levels of kurtosis, it has a leptokurtic shape (more cases in the tails); when a distribution has low levels of kurtosis, it has a platykurtic shape (fewer cases in the tails).</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Skew</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The extent to which a distribution departs from being symmetrical, as a result of being pulled by outliers on one side of a distribution. A normal distribution has zero skew; positive skew means outliers exist on the right-side of the distribution, so the left-side curve and tail are clustered. Negative skew means outliers exist on the left-side of the distribution, so right-side curve and tail are clustered.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.4. Unit 2 Symbols and Notation</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Symbol or </strong><strong>Notation</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Description</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG11&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mu" title="\mu" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Greek letter mu, the symbol for the population mean</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x}" title="\bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">“x-bar,” the symbol for the sample mean</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XFNpZ21h&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\Sigma" title="\Sigma" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Greek uppercase letter sigma, the symbol for sum. When you see this, you use addition.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x" title="x" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>x</em> represents each variable value in your data set.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=Tg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="N" title="N" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>N</em> represents the number of cases or people in your population or sample.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfeA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_x" title="SS_x" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Notation for sum of squares of <em>x</em></p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21hXjI&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma^2" title="\sigma^2" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Population variance</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c14y&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s^2" title="s^2" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Sample variance</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21h&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma" title="\sigma" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Population standard deviation</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><span style="font-size: NaNpt; color: #; text-decoration: none;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=cw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s" title="s" class="latex mathjax" /></span></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Sample standard deviation</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table class="grid"><caption>Table C.5. Unit 2 Formulas for Populations and Samples</caption> <thead><tr><th class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;" scope="col"><strong>Formulas</strong></th> <th class="shaded" style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;" scope="col"><strong>Population Parameters</strong></th> <th class="shaded" style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;" scope="col"><strong>Sample Statistics</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr><td class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;">Mean</td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG11ID0gXGZyYWN7XHN1bSB4fXtOfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mu = \frac{\sum x}{N}" title="\mu = \frac{\sum x}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fSA9IFxmcmFje1xzdW0geH17bn0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x} = \frac{\sum x}{n}" title="\bar{x} = \frac{\sum x}{n}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr><td class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;">Deviation score</td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=KHgg4oCTIFxtdSk&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="(x – \mu)" title="(x – \mu)" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=KHgg4oCTIFxiYXJ7eH0p&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="(x – \bar{x})" title="(x – \bar{x})" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr><td class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;">Sum of squares (Definitional)</td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG1hdGhiZntcU2lnbWF9KHgg4oCTIFxtdSleMg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mathbf{\Sigma}(x – \mu)^2" title="\mathbf{\Sigma}(x – \mu)^2" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG1hdGhiZntcU2lnbWF9KHgg4oCTIFxiYXJ7eH0pXjI&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mathbf{\Sigma}(x – \bar{x})^2" title="\mathbf{\Sigma}(x – \bar{x})^2" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr><td class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;">Sum of squares (Computational)</td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfeCA9IFxzdW0geF4yIC0gXGZyYWN7KFxzdW0geCleMn17Tn0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_x = \sum x^2 - \frac{(\sum x)^2}{N}" title="SS_x = \sum x^2 - \frac{(\sum x)^2}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfeCA9IFxzdW0geF4yIC0gXGZyYWN7KFxzdW0geCleMn17Tn0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_x = \sum x^2 - \frac{(\sum x)^2}{N}" title="SS_x = \sum x^2 - \frac{(\sum x)^2}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr><td class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;">Variance</td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21hXjIgPSBcZnJhY3tTU194fXtOfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma^2 = \frac{SS_x}{N}" title="\sigma^2 = \frac{SS_x}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c14yID0gXGZyYWN7U1NfeH17KE4tMSl9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s^2 = \frac{SS_x}{(N-1)}" title="s^2 = \frac{SS_x}{(N-1)}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr><td class="shaded" style="width: 221.516px;">Standard deviation</td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21hID0gXHNxcnR7XHNpZ21hXjJ9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2}" title="\sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="text-align: center; width: 368.938px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=cyA9IFxzcXJ0e3NeMn0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s = \sqrt{s^2}" title="s = \sqrt{s^2}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 class="import-Normal">Unit 3: Standard Normal Distributions and <em>z</em>-Scores</h2> <p>Tables C.6–C.9 summarize key concepts, notations, symbols, and equations from Unit 3.</p> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.6. Unit 3 Concepts</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Concept</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Definition or Details</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Standard normal distribution</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">This distribution has a mean of 0 (at the center of the distribution), and a standard deviation of 1 (lines going above and below the mean at the center of the distribution are in increments of 1). Thus, if a <em>z</em>-score is positive, it is above the mean (of 0). If a <em>z</em>-score is negative, it is below the mean (of 0). Thus, you can transform any raw scores into <em>z</em>-scores, plot them on the standard normal distribution, and then easily make comparisons because they are on the same standard scale.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>z</em>-score</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">A standardized score based on a raw score (<em>x</em>), population or sample mean (𝜇 or <em><span style="font-size: NaNpt; color: #; text-decoration: none;">x̅</span></em>), and standard deviation (𝜎 or <em>s</em>) that you are comparing it to. A <em>z</em>-score is scaled in terms of standard deviations and is plotted on the standard normal distribution.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Interpreting <em>z</em>-scores (probability and proportion)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The <em>z</em>-score can be further interpreted in terms of probability and proportion; you can use Table A.1 in the Appendix to look up areas between your <em>z</em>-score and the mean, as well as the area beyond your <em>z</em>-score. These areas can be used to answer questions about probability (e.g., what is the probability of scoring higher than a positive <em>z-</em>score?<em>)</em> or the proportion of a population with scores less than a particular <em>z-score.</em></p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Percentile scores</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Percentage of scores that fall below the observed score</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Cut-off scores</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">With cut-off scores, we start with the percentage we are interested in and work backwards to find the <em>z</em>-score and raw score (<em>x</em>) that are associated with the top or bottom percentage.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.7. Unit 3 Symbols and Notation</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Symbol or </strong><strong>Notation</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Description</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x" title="x" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">This is the score that you want to convert.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG11IFw6IFx0ZXh0e29yfVw6IFxiYXJ7eH0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mu \: \text{or}\: \bar{x}" title="\mu \: \text{or}\: \bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">This is the mean of the group you are comparing your participants’ scores to.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21hIFw6IFx0ZXh0e29yfVw6IHM&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma \: \text{or}\: s" title="\sigma \: \text{or}\: s" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">This is the standard deviation of the group you are comparing your participants’ scores to.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="z" title="z" class="latex mathjax" /></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Unit 4 covers the calculation of <em>z</em>-scores; they are scaled in terms of standard deviations and are plotted on the standard normal distribution.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>&nbsp;</p> <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 20px;"><caption><strong>Table C.8. Unit 3 Formulas for Populations and Samples&nbsp;</strong></caption> <thead><tr style="background-color: #e7e6e6;"><th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Formulas</th> <th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: center; width: 40%;">Population Parameters</th> <th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: center; width: 40%;">Sample Statistics</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">z-score</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eiA9IFxmcmFjeyh4IC0gXG11KX17XHNpZ21hfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="z = \frac{(x - \mu)}{\sigma}" title="z = \frac{(x - \mu)}{\sigma}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eiA9IFxmcmFjeyh4IC0gXGJhcnt4fSl9e3N9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="z = \frac{(x - \bar{x})}{s}" title="z = \frac{(x - \bar{x})}{s}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Cutoff score</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eCA9IHooXHNpZ21hKSArIFxtdQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x = z(\sigma) + \mu" title="x = z(\sigma) + \mu" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eCA9IHoocykgKyBcYmFye3h9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x = z(s) + \bar{x}" title="x = z(s) + \bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <hr /> <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 20px;"><caption><strong>Table C.9. Unit 3 Steps for Populations and Samples&nbsp;</strong></caption> <thead><tr style="background-color: #e7e6e6;"><th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Score Type</th> <th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 80%;">Steps</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; vertical-align: top; background-color: #f9f9f9;" rowspan="3">Percentile score</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 1:</strong> <strong>Negative <em>z</em>-score:</strong> Look up the <em>area beyond z</em> in Table A.1 to find the proportion of cases that fall below this negative <em>z</em>-score.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 2:</strong> <strong>Positive <em>z</em>-score:</strong> Look up the <em>area between the mean and z</em> + .50 to find the proportion of cases that fall below a positive <em>z</em>-score.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 3:</strong> If a <em>z</em>-score equals 0, that means that it is at the 50<sup>th</sup> percentile.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; vertical-align: top; background-color: #f9f9f9;" rowspan="4">Cutoff score</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Start with the percentage or proportion of cases you are interested in working with.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Using Table A.1, find the <em>area beyond z</em> that is as close to that proportion as possible.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 3:</strong> Find the affiliated <em>z</em>-score in column 1 of Table A.1.<span class="footnote"><span class="footnote-indirect" data-fnref="308-1"></span></span></td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><strong>Step 4:</strong> Plug the values into your equation to solve for <em>x </em>using: <div style="margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eCA9IHooXHNpZ21hKSArIFxtdQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x = z(\sigma) + \mu" title="x = z(\sigma) + \mu" class="latex mathjax" /></div> <p style="margin: 4px 0;"><em>or</em></p> <div style="margin-top: 10px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eCA9IHoocykgKyBcYmFye3h9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x = z(s) + \bar{x}" title="x = z(s) + \bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /></div> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2>Unit 4: Introduction to Inferential Statistics and Hypothesis Testing</h2> <p>Tables C.10–C.12 and Key Takeaways C.1 summarize key concepts, notations, symbols, and equations from Unit 4.</p> <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;"><caption><strong>Table C.10. Unit 4 Concepts </strong></caption> <thead><tr style="background-color: #e7e6e6;"><th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 25%;"><strong>Concept</strong></th> <th style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 75%;"><strong>Definition or Details</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Inferential statistics</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Allow us to draw conclusions about populations based on samples randomly selected (i.e., sampled by methods of chance, such as drawing names from a hat) from these populations</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Sampling distribution</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">A distribution that results from taking an infinite number of random samples of the same size from the same population. This sampling distribution is used in all inferential statistics.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; vertical-align: top; background-color: #f9f9f9;" rowspan="3">Central limit theorem</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><em>Shape</em>: As the sample size increases, the shape of the sampling distribution approximates normality (approximates the normal distribution), which allows us to use statistical analyses based on the normal distribution (see Chapter 7).</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><em>Mean: </em>The mean of the sampling distribution equals the mean in the population from which samples were randomly selected.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;"><em>Variability: </em>The standard deviation of the sampling distribution decreases as the sample size increases; in other words, larger samples lead to more accurate estimates (with less sampling error). We can compute this expected sampling error (also known as the standard error).</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Sampling error</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Difference between our sample statistic and population parameter</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Standard error</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Expected sampling error</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Confidence intervals</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Provide us with a way to assess our level of confidence in representing the population; they can tell us with 95% or 99% confidence that we have represented the population. Thus, confidence intervals give us a range within which a population parameter is located.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">One-sample z-test</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Compares one sample mean to a population mean and standard deviation</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Confidence</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">This is the total area of the distribution that is not in the tail or tails of a distribution. Commonly, you’ll see people set their confidence level at 95%, but you can also set it to 99% or another confidence level.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Alpha (α)</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">This is the total area of the distribution that is in the tail or tails of a distribution. Because the alpha and confidence areas add up to 1.00 or 100%, your alpha area would be set at 0.05 or 5%.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Critical value(s)</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Value that separates your confidence and alpha areas of a distribution.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; background-color: #f9f9f9; vertical-align: top;" rowspan="2">Probability values (“<em>p </em>values”)</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Probability values, or “<em>p</em> values,” are computed alphas. When the <em>p</em> value is less than the alpha you set, you reject the null hypothesis.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">Probability values, or “<em>p</em> values,” are computed alphas. When the <em>p</em> value is greater than the alpha you set, you fail to reject the null hypothesis.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Type I error</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">This is when you say there is a difference (“reject the null hypothesis”), but there actually isn’t a difference. You may see this referred to as a <em>false positive</em>.</td> </tr> <tr><td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px; font-weight: bold;">Type II error</td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid #000000; padding: 10px;">This is when you say there is no difference (“fail to reject the null hypothesis”), but there actually is a difference. You may see this referred to as a <em>false negative</em>.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>&nbsp;</p> <div class="textbox textbox--key-takeaways"><div class="textbox__header"><p class="textbox__title">Key Takeaways C.1. Unit 4: Hypothesis Testing Steps</p> </div> <div class="textbox__content"><ol><li class="import-Normal"><strong>Hypothesis Pair</strong> <ul><li>Null hypothesis, <em>H</em><sub>o</sub>: Statement of no difference</li> <li>Alternative hypothesis, <em>H</em><sub>a</sub>: Statement of difference</li> </ul> </li> <li><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Setup for the Test</strong></p> <ul><li>What inferential test are you using?</li> <li>Set your confidence/alpha.</li> <li>Find your critical value(s).</li> </ul> </li> <li><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Test Statistic computation</strong></p> <ul><li>Solve for your obtained value.</li> </ul> </li> <li><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Make a decision</strong></p> <ul><li>Reject the null hypothesis.</li> <li>Fail to reject the null hypothesis.</li> </ul> </li> </ol> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </div> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.11. Unit 4 Symbols and Notation</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Symbol or </strong><strong>Notation</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Description</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG11X3tcYmFye3h9fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mu_{\bar{x}}" title="\mu_{\bar{x}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Sampling distribution mean</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHN1bSBcYmFye3h9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sum \bar{x}" title="\sum \bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Sum of <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x}" title="\bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /> values (from simulations of central limit theorem)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG11ID0gXG11X3tcYmFye3h9fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mu = \mu_{\bar{x}}" title="\mu = \mu_{\bar{x}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The mean of the population equals the mean of the sampling distribution.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21hX3tcYmFye3h9fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma_{\bar{x}}" title="\sigma_{\bar{x}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Population standard error</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c197XGJhcnt4fX0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_{\bar{x}}" title="s_{\bar{x}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Sample standard error</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=SF9v&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="H_o" title="H_o" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Null hypothesis</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=SF9h&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="H_a" title="H_a" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Alternative hypothesis</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XG11XzE&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\mu_1" title="\mu_1" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Mu (population mean): Used when testing an inferential test; we are making hypotheses and inferences about a population.</p> <p class="import-Normal">There is a subscript “1”–this is because we are taking one sample and comparing its mean to the population mean.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=PSBcdGV4dHsgb3IgfSBcbmVx&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="= \text{ or } \neq" title="= \text{ or } \neq" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">In two-tailed hypothesis testing, we will be testing to see if there is a difference (≠) or no difference (=). One-tailed tests will use “&lt;” or “&gt;” instead of “≠.”</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGFscGhh&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\alpha" title="\alpha" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">This is the total area of the distribution that is in the tail or tails of a distribution. Because alpha and confidence areas add up to 1.00 or 100%, your alpha area would be set at 0.05 or 5%.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table class="grid" style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; height: 178px;"><caption>Table C.12. Unit 4 Formulas for Populations and Samples</caption> <thead><tr style="height: 35px;"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 35px; width: 33.3333%;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Formula</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 35px; width: 33.3333%;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Population Parameters</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; height: 35px; width: 33.3333%;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Sample Statistics</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr style="height: 70px;"><th style="width: 33.3333%; height: 70px;" scope="row"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Standard error </strong><strong>(expected sampling error)</strong></p> </th> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 70px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHNpZ21hX3tcYmFye3h9fSA9IFxmcmFje1xzaWdtYX17XHNxcnR7Tn19&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\sigma_{\bar{x}} = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{N}}" title="\sigma_{\bar{x}} = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{N}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 70px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c197XGJhcnt4fX0gPSBcZnJhY3tzfXtcc3FydHtOfX0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_{\bar{x}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{N}}" title="s_{\bar{x}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{N}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr style="height: 18px;"><th style="width: 33.3333%; height: 18px;" scope="row"><em>z</em>-test (<em>z</em>-obtained)</th> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 18px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eiA9IFxmcmFjeyhcYmFye3h9IC0gXG11KX17XHNpZ21hX3tcYmFye3h9fX0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="z = \frac{(\bar{x} - \mu)}{\sigma_{\bar{x}}}" title="z = \frac{(\bar{x} - \mu)}{\sigma_{\bar{x}}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 18px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eiA9IFxmcmFjeyhcYmFye3h9IC0gXG11KX17XHNpZ21hX3tcYmFye3h9fX0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="z = \frac{(\bar{x} - \mu)}{\sigma_{\bar{x}}}" title="z = \frac{(\bar{x} - \mu)}{\sigma_{\bar{x}}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr style="height: 37px;"><th style="width: 33.3333%; height: 37px;" scope="row">95% confidence interval (CI)</th> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 37px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHRleHR7OTVcJSBDb25maWRlbmNlIEludGVydmFsIChDSSkgb2YgfSBcbXUgPSBcYmFye3h9IFxwbSAxLjk2KFxzaWdtYV97XGJhcnt4fX0p&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\text{95\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 1.96(\sigma_{\bar{x}})" title="\text{95\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 1.96(\sigma_{\bar{x}})" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 37px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHRleHR7OTVcJSBDb25maWRlbmNlIEludGVydmFsIChDSSkgb2YgfSBcbXUgPSBcYmFye3h9IFxwbSAxLjk2KHNfe1xiYXJ7eH19KQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\text{95\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 1.96(s_{\bar{x}})" title="\text{95\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 1.96(s_{\bar{x}})" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr style="height: 18px;"><th style="width: 33.3333%; height: 18px;" scope="row">99% confidence interval (CI)</th> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 18px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHRleHR7OTlcJSBDb25maWRlbmNlIEludGVydmFsIChDSSkgb2YgfSBcbXUgPSBcYmFye3h9IFxwbSAyLjU4KFxzaWdtYV97XGJhcnt4fX0p&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\text{99\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 2.58(\sigma_{\bar{x}})" title="\text{99\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 2.58(\sigma_{\bar{x}})" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td style="width: 33.3333%; height: 18px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHRleHR7OTlcJSBDb25maWRlbmNlIEludGVydmFsIChDSSkgb2YgfSBcbXUgPSBcYmFye3h9IFxwbSAyLjU4KHNfe1xiYXJ7eH19KQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\text{99\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 2.58(s_{\bar{x}})" title="\text{99\% Confidence Interval (CI) of } \mu = \bar{x} \pm 2.58(s_{\bar{x}})" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2>Unit 5: Inferential Statistics: Looking at Differences</h2> <p>Tables C.13–C.16 summarize key concepts, notations, symbols, and equations from Unit 5.</p> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.13. Unit 5 Concepts</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Concept</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Definition or Details</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>t</em> distribution</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Area of 1.00 under the bell-shaped curve; the mean is still 0 at the center of your distribution. If your sample size is around 100 or greater, the shape of your distribution looks normal. However, if your sample size is smaller (typically less than 100), the shape changes slightly as a function of your degrees of freedom. Thus, <em>t</em> distributions are a family of distributions; each distribution looks slightly different for each different degree of freedom.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Degrees of freedom (<em>df</em>)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The number of observations or values in the data that are free to vary</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">One-sample <em>t</em>-test</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Allows you to compare one sample mean to a population mean (when population standard deviation is unknown and/or you have a small sample)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Independent samples <em>t</em>-test</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Allows you to compare differences between two independent sample means (when you have a categorical independent variable with two groups and one continuous dependent variable)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Dependent samples <em>t</em>-test</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Allows you to compare differences within two dependent sample means (when you have a categorical independent variable with two dependent groups [e.g., time], and one continuous dependent variable)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">One-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Allows you to examine differences between three or more categories of one independent variable on a continuous dependent variable</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Between-groups&nbsp; variability</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Examine differences between the group means for an analysis of variance (ANOVA) test</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Within-groups&nbsp; variability</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Examine differences within each group for an ANOVA test</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>F </em>distribution</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Scaled on variance rather than standard deviation like the <em>t</em> distribution and standard normal distribution. Thus, the distribution starts at 0, peaks at 1, and then extends a tail for positive critical and obtained values. The <em>F</em> distribution is a family of distributions, where the characteristics and shape vary according to degrees of freedom.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>F</em> (ratio)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The <em>F-</em>obtained value is the ratio of between-groups to within-groups variability. If the ratio is large, then there is more variability between groups, which indicates more of an effect or difference between groups. If the ratio is small, then there is less of a difference, and there is more room for error within a group. If the ratio is 1, then the groups are identical.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.14. Unit 5 Symbols and Notation</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Symbol or </strong><strong>Notation</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Description</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHRleHR7ZGZ9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\text{df}" title="\text{df}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Degrees of freedom</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2FsaWduZWR9IEhfMCY6IFxtdV8xID0gXG11XzIgXFwgSF9hJjogXG11XzEgXG5lcSBcbXVfMiBcZW5ke2FsaWduZWR9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_1 = \mu_2 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_1 \neq \mu_2 \end{aligned}" title="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_1 = \mu_2 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_1 \neq \mu_2 \end{aligned}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Hypothesis pair statements for an independent samples <em>t</em>-test</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2FsaWduZWR9IEhfMCY6IFxtdV9BIC0gXG11X0IgPSAwIFxcIEhfYSY6IFxtdV9BIC0gXG11X0IgXG5lcSAwIFxlbmR7YWxpZ25lZH0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_A - \mu_B = 0 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_A - \mu_B \neq 0 \end{aligned}" title="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_A - \mu_B = 0 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_A - \mu_B \neq 0 \end{aligned}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Hypothesis pair statements for dependent samples <em>t</em>-test</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2FsaWduZWR9IEhfMCY6IFxtdV9EID0gMCBcXCBIX2EmOiBcbXVfRCBcbmVxIDAgXGVuZHthbGlnbmVkfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_D = 0 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_D \neq 0 \end{aligned}" title="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_D = 0 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_D \neq 0 \end{aligned}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">(Another option): Hypothesis pair statements for dependent samples <em>t-</em>test</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=aw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="k" title="k" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Number of levels or categorical groups</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2FsaWduZWR9IEhfMCY6IFxtdV8xID0gXG11XzIgPSBcbXVfMyBcXCBIX2EmOiBcbXVfMSBcbmVxIFxtdV8yIFxuZXEgXG11XzMgXGVuZHthbGlnbmVkfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_1 = \mu_2 = \mu_3 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_1 \neq \mu_2 \neq \mu_3 \end{aligned}" title="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \mu_1 = \mu_2 = \mu_3 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \mu_1 \neq \mu_2 \neq \mu_3 \end{aligned}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Hypothesis pair statements for one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA; with three levels)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfYiwgU1NfdywgU1NfdA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_b, SS_w, SS_t" title="SS_b, SS_w, SS_t" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Notation for sum of squares between groups, within groups, and total (one-way ANOVA)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=ZGZfYiwgZGZfdywgZGZfdA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="df_b, df_w, df_t" title="df_b, df_w, df_t" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Notation for degrees of freedom between groups, within groups, and total (one-way ANOVA)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=TVNfYiwgTVNfdw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="MS_b, MS_w" title="MS_b, MS_w" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Notation for mean squares between and within groups (one-way ANOVA)</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 860px; height: 756px;"><caption>Table C.15. Unit 5 Formulas&nbsp;</caption> <thead><tr style="height: 24.25pt;"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Formulas</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; height: 35px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Statistics</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 22.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Standard error</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c197XGJhcnt4fX0gPSBcZnJhY3tzfXtcc3FydHtOfX0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_{\bar{x}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{N}}" title="s_{\bar{x}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{N}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 25.6pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>z</em>-test</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eiA9IFxmcmFjeyhcYmFye3h9IC0gXG11KX17c197XGJhcnt4fX19&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="z = \frac{(\bar{x} - \mu)}{s_{\bar{x}}}" title="z = \frac{(\bar{x} - \mu)}{s_{\bar{x}}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Pooled variance (independent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c19wXjIgPSBcZnJhY3soU1NfMSArIFNTXzIpfXsoZGZfMSArIGRmXzIpfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_p^2 = \frac{(SS_1 + SS_2)}{(df_1 + df_2)}" title="s_p^2 = \frac{(SS_1 + SS_2)}{(df_1 + df_2)}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 25.6pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Standard error (pooled variance independent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #ffffff; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c197XGJhcnt4fV8xIC0gXGJhcnt4fV8yfSA9IFxzcXJ0e1xmcmFje3NfcF4yfXtOXzF9ICsgXGZyYWN7c19wXjJ9e05fMn19&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2} = \sqrt{\frac{s_p^2}{N_1} + \frac{s_p^2}{N_2}}" title="s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2} = \sqrt{\frac{s_p^2}{N_1} + \frac{s_p^2}{N_2}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>t</em>-obtained (pooled variance independent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=dCA9IFxmcmFjeyhcYmFye3h9XzEgLSBcYmFye3h9XzIpfXtzX3tcYmFye3h9XzEgLSBcYmFye3h9XzJ9fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="t = \frac{(\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2)}{s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2}}" title="t = \frac{(\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2)}{s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Variance (separate variance independent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2dhdGhlcmVkfSBzXzFeMiA9IFxmcmFje1NTXzF9e2RmXzF9IFxcIHNfMl4yID0gXGZyYWN7U1NfMn17ZGZfMn0gXGVuZHtnYXRoZXJlZH0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{gathered} s_1^2 = \frac{SS_1}{df_1} \\ s_2^2 = \frac{SS_2}{df_2} \end{gathered}" title="\begin{gathered} s_1^2 = \frac{SS_1}{df_1} \\ s_2^2 = \frac{SS_2}{df_2} \end{gathered}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Standard error (separate variance independent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c197XGJhcnt4fV8xIC0gXGJhcnt4fV8yfSA9IFxzcXJ0e1xmcmFje3NfMV4yfXtOXzF9ICsgXGZyYWN7c18yXjJ9e05fMn19&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2} = \sqrt{\frac{s_1^2}{N_1} + \frac{s_2^2}{N_2}}" title="s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2} = \sqrt{\frac{s_1^2}{N_1} + \frac{s_2^2}{N_2}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>t</em>-obtained (separate variance independent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=dCA9IFxmcmFjeyhcYmFye3h9XzEgLSBcYmFye3h9XzIpfXtzX3tcYmFye3h9XzEgLSBcYmFye3h9XzJ9fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="t = \frac{(\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2)}{s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2}}" title="t = \frac{(\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2)}{s_{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Average differences between paired scores (dependent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fV9EID0gXGZyYWN7XHN1bSBEfXtOfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x}_D = \frac{\sum D}{N}" title="\bar{x}_D = \frac{\sum D}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Standard error (dependent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=c197XGJhcnt4fV9EfSA9IFxmcmFje3NfRH17XHNxcnR7Tn19&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="s_{\bar{x}_D} = \frac{s_D}{\sqrt{N}}" title="s_{\bar{x}_D} = \frac{s_D}{\sqrt{N}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>t</em>-obtained (dependent samples <em>t</em>-test)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=dCA9IFxmcmFje1xiYXJ7eH1fRH17c197XGJhcnt4fV9EfX0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="t = \frac{\bar{x}_D}{s_{\bar{x}_D}}" title="t = \frac{\bar{x}_D}{s_{\bar{x}_D}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Between-groups sum of squares (analysis of variance [ANOVA])</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfYiA9IFxsZWZ0KFxzdW1fe2k9MX1ee2t9IFxmcmFjeyhcc3VtIHhfaSleMn17Tl9pfVxyaWdodCkgLSBcZnJhY3soXHN1bSB4X3QpXjJ9e05fdH0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_b = \left(\sum_{i=1}^{k} \frac{(\sum x_i)^2}{N_i}\right) - \frac{(\sum x_t)^2}{N_t}" title="SS_b = \left(\sum_{i=1}^{k} \frac{(\sum x_i)^2}{N_i}\right) - \frac{(\sum x_t)^2}{N_t}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Within-groups sum of squares (ANOVA)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2dhdGhlcmVkfSBTU193ID0gXHN1bV97aT0xfV57a30gXGxlZnQoXHN1bSB4X2leMiAtIFxmcmFjeyhcc3VtIHhfaSleMn17Tl9pfVxyaWdodCkgXFwgXHRleHR7QWxzbyBub3RhdGVkOn0gXFwgU1NfdyA9IFNTXzEgKyBTU18yICsgU1NfMyBcZW5ke2dhdGhlcmVkfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{gathered} SS_w = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \left(\sum x_i^2 - \frac{(\sum x_i)^2}{N_i}\right) \\ \text{Also notated:} \\ SS_w = SS_1 + SS_2 + SS_3 \end{gathered}" title="\begin{gathered} SS_w = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \left(\sum x_i^2 - \frac{(\sum x_i)^2}{N_i}\right) \\ \text{Also notated:} \\ SS_w = SS_1 + SS_2 + SS_3 \end{gathered}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Total sum of squares (ANOVA)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfdCA9IFxzdW0geF90XjIgLSBcZnJhY3soXHN1bSB4X3QpXjJ9e05fdH0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_t = \sum x_t^2 - \frac{(\sum x_t)^2}{N_t}" title="SS_t = \sum x_t^2 - \frac{(\sum x_t)^2}{N_t}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>df</em> between groups (ANOVA)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=ZGZfYiA9IGsgLSAx&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="df_b = k - 1" title="df_b = k - 1" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>df</em> within groups (ANOVA)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=ZGZfdyA9IE5fdCAtIGs&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="df_w = N_t - k" title="df_w = N_t - k" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>df</em> total (ANOVA)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=ZGZfdCA9IE5fdCAtIDE&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="df_t = N_t - 1" title="df_t = N_t - 1" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Mean squares between groups</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=TVNfYiA9IFxmcmFje1NTX2J9e2RmX2J9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="MS_b = \frac{SS_b}{df_b}" title="MS_b = \frac{SS_b}{df_b}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal">Mean squares within groups</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=TVNfdyA9IFxmcmFje1NTX3d9e2RmX3d9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="MS_w = \frac{SS_w}{df_w}" title="MS_w = \frac{SS_w}{df_w}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 27.4pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 522.297px; height: 35px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>F</em>-obtained</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 304.516px; text-align: center; height: 35px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=RiA9IFxmcmFje01TX2J9e01TX3d9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="F = \frac{MS_b}{MS_w}" title="F = \frac{MS_b}{MS_w}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 class="import-Normal">Unit 6: Inferential Statistics: Looking at Relationships</h2> <p>Table C.16–C.18 summarizes key concepts, notations, symbols, and equations from Unit 6.</p> <table style="width: 476.75pt;"><caption>Table C.16. Unit 6 Concepts</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Concept</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Definition or Details</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Correlation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Degree of a linear relationship between two continuous variables. A linear relationship is one that can be described by a straight line. Correlation provides you with an estimate of covariance, which is a measure of how two variables move.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Pearson’s <em>r</em></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Correlation between two continuous variables can be tested using Pearson’s <em>r</em>. This test provides you with a value between −1 and 1.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Positive correlation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">(Pearson’s <em>r</em> value between &gt;0 and 1). These variables move in the same direction. This tells you:</p> <ul><li>The variables are moving in the same direction</li> <li>The closer the value is to 1, the stronger the magnitude.</li> <li>A correlation of 1 is a perfect positive correlation.</li> </ul> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Negative correlation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">(Pearson’s <em>r</em> value between −1 and &lt;0). These variables move in the opposite direction. This tells you:</p> <ul><li>The variables are moving in opposite directions</li> <li>The closer the value is to −1, the stronger the magnitude.</li> <li>A correlation of −1 is a perfect negative correlation.</li> </ul> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">No correlation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Pearson’s <em>r</em> value of 0, which means that there is no meaningful relationship between the two variables</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Correlation does not imply causation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">We can see whether the variables move together in the same direction or in opposite directions, but we do not know if one variable is causing the other variable to move in this way.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Linear regression</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The goal is to make a prediction, using the line equation (<em>y = mx + b</em>). So, if we know something about <em>x</em> (the predictor), we can then estimate the value of <em>y</em> (the outcome). You will be estimating a line to help in making these predictive estimates. You will be estimating a line that best fits your data points.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Slope</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The average change in <em>y</em> for every 1-unit increase in <em>x</em>. You may see the slope called the <em>rate of change</em> or described as the <em>rise over run</em> (i.e., how much the line goes up or down and how much the line moves over horizontally). In statistics, you may also see this referred to as the <em>regression coefficient</em>, which quantifies the direction and magnitude of the relationship between your independent and dependent variables.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>y</em>-intercept</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The predicted value of <em>y</em> when <em>x</em> is equal to 0 (i.e., when the line crosses the <em>y</em>-axis)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Residual</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">The distance between the estimated line and each data point</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Coefficient of determination</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">A way to quantify a residual with an <em>r</em><sup>2 </sup>value between 0 and 1; the closer to 1 that your <em>r</em><sup>2</sup> is, the better your predictions are (with smaller amounts of residuals). The closer to 0 that your <em>r</em><sup>2</sup> is, the less helpful your predictions are (with more residuals).</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">Chi-Square (χ<sup>2</sup>) tests: goodness-of-fit and test for independence</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: solid windowtext 0.5pt;"><p class="import-Normal">This statistic examines frequency distributions when you have nominal (categorical) data. You can test to see whether there is a significant difference between what you observe and what you would expect in a frequency distribution. There is a chi-square goodness-of-fit test, which focuses on a univariate frequency distribution. There is also a chi-square test for independence, which examines the association between two categorical variables.</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 715px;"><caption>Table C.17. Unit 6 Symbols and Notation</caption> <thead><tr><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Symbol or </strong><strong>Notation</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Description</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJlZ2lue2FsaWduZWR9IEhfMCY6IFxyaG8gPSAwIFxcIEhfYSY6IFxyaG8gXG5lcSAwIFxlbmR7YWxpZ25lZH0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \rho = 0 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \rho \neq 0 \end{aligned}" title="\begin{aligned} H_0&amp;amp;: \rho = 0 \\ H_a&amp;amp;: \rho \neq 0 \end{aligned}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Hypothesis pair for a correlation (Pearson’s <em>r</em>) test</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XHJobw&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\rho" title="\rho" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Population correlation (Greek letter rho)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1Bfe3h5fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SP_{xy}" title="SP_{xy}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Sum of products for <em>x</em> and <em>y</em> (i.e., the amount of covariance you have)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=cg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="r" title="r" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Pearson’s <em>r</em> value (quantifies your correlation)</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGhhdHt5fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\hat{y}" title="\hat{y}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">“y-hat,” the predicted value of your outcome</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=YQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="a" title="a" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Estimated <em>y</em>-intercept, which is the predicted value of <em>y</em> when <em>x</em> is equal to 0 (i.e., when the line crosses the <em>y</em>-axis).</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=Yg&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="b" title="b" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Estimated slope, which represents the average change in <em>y</em> for every 1-unit increase in <em>x</em>. You may see the slope called the <em>rate of change</em> or described as the <em>rise over run</em> (i.e., how much the line goes up or down, and how much the line moves over horizontally).</p> <p class="import-Normal">In statistics, you may also see this referred to as the <em>regression coefficient</em>, which quantifies the direction and magnitude of the relationship between your independent and dependent variables.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="x" title="x" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Your predictor variable (independent variable) value</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=eQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="y" title="y" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Your outcome variable (dependent variable) value</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fSBcdGV4dHsgYW5kIH0gXGJhcnt5fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x} \text{ and } \bar{y}" title="\bar{x} \text{ and } \bar{y}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Means for your <em>x</em> and <em>y</em> variables.</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGNoaV4y&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\chi^2" title="\chi^2" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Chi-square</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 169.375px; text-align: center;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=TyBcdGV4dHsgYW5kIH0gRQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="O \text{ and } E" title="O \text{ and } E" class="latex mathjax" /></td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 512.438px;"><p class="import-Normal">Observed and expected values (in chi-square tests)</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <table style="width: 759px; height: 479px;"><caption>Table C.18. Unit 6 Formulas</caption> <thead><tr style="height: 24.25pt;"><th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 35px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal"><strong>Formulas</strong></p> </th> <th class="TableGrid-C" style="background-color: #e7e6e6; vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; height: 35px;" scope="col"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;"><strong>Statistics</strong></p> </th> </tr> </thead> <tbody><tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 36.85pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 70px;"><p class="import-Normal">Sum of squares for <em>x</em></p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 70px;">&nbsp; <div><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfeCA9IFxzdW0geF4yIC0gXGZyYWN7KFxzdW0geCleMn17Tn0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_x = \sum x^2 - \frac{(\sum x)^2}{N}" title="SS_x = \sum x^2 - \frac{(\sum x)^2}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></div> </td> </tr> <tr style="height: 36px;"><td style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 36px;"><p class="import-Normal">Sum of squares for <em>y</em></p> </td> <td style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 36px;"><div><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1NfeSA9IFxzdW0geV4yIC0gXGZyYWN7KFxzdW0geSleMn17Tn0&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SS_y = \sum y^2 - \frac{(\sum y)^2}{N}" title="SS_y = \sum y^2 - \frac{(\sum y)^2}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></div> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 22.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 36px;"><p class="import-Normal">Sum of the products for <em>x</em> and <em>y</em> (<em>SP<sub>xy</sub></em>)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 36px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=U1Bfe3h5fSA9IFxzdW0geHkgLSBcZnJhY3soXHN1bSB4KShcc3VtIHkpfXtOfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="SP_{xy} = \sum xy - \frac{(\sum x)(\sum y)}{N}" title="SP_{xy} = \sum xy - \frac{(\sum x)(\sum y)}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 25.6pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 36px;"><p class="import-Normal">Pearson’s <em>r</em> (obtained value for correlation)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 36px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=ciA9IFxmcmFje1NQX3t4eX19e1xzcXJ0eyhTU194KShTU195KX19&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="r = \frac{SP_{xy}}{\sqrt{(SS_x)(SS_y)}}" title="r = \frac{SP_{xy}}{\sqrt{(SS_x)(SS_y)}}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Slope</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=YiA9IFxmcmFje1NQX3t4eX19e1NTX3h9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="b = \frac{SP_{xy}}{SS_x}" title="b = \frac{SP_{xy}}{SS_x}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal"><em>y</em>-intercept</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=YSA9IFxiYXJ7eX0gLSAoYilcYmFye3h9&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="a = \bar{y} - (b)\bar{x}" title="a = \bar{y} - (b)\bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Linear equation</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGhhdHt5fSA9IGEgKyBieA&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\hat{y} = a + bx" title="\hat{y} = a + bx" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Residual</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGhhdHt5fSAtIHk&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\hat{y} - y" title="\hat{y} - y" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Coefficient of determination</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=cl4y&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="r^2" title="r^2" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Chi-square test</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGNoaV4yID0gXHN1bSBcbGVmdChcZnJhY3soTyAtIEUpXjJ9e0V9XHJpZ2h0KQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\chi^2 = \sum \left(\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}\right)" title="\chi^2 = \sum \left(\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}\right)" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> <tr class="TableGrid-R" style="height: 40.9pt;"><td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 398.26px; height: 38px;"><p class="import-Normal">Expected frequency (for chi-square test of independence)</p> </td> <td class="TableGrid-C" style="vertical-align: middle; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 328.531px; text-align: center; height: 38px;"><img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=Rl9lID0gXGZyYWN7XHRleHR7KHJvdyB0b3RhbCl9KFx0ZXh0e2NvbHVtbiB0b3RhbH0pfXtOfQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="F_e = \frac{\text{(row total)}(\text{column total})}{N}" title="F_e = \frac{\text{(row total)}(\text{column total})}{N}" class="latex mathjax" /></td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div>
	</div>
			
				<div class="footnotes"><div id='308-1'><p>This will be a positive z-score if you are interested in the top percentage and a negative z-score if you are interested in a bottom percentage.</p>
</div></div>
	</div>

</div>
