 border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">60</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">79.082</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">88.379</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">65</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">84.821</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">94.422</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">70</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">90.531</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">100.425</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">75</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">96.217</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">106.393</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">80</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">101.879</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">112.329</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">85</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">107.522</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">118.236</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">90</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">113.145</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">124.116</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">95</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">118.752</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">129.973</p> </td> </tr> <tr class="TableNormal-R" style="height: 13.55pt;"><td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: bottom; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 101.133px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">100</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="background-color: #e8e8e8; vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 191.602px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">124.342</p> </td> <td class="TableNormal-C" style="vertical-align: middle; padding: 0pt 5.4pt; border: 0.5pt solid windowtext; width: 192.094px;"><p class="import-Normal" style="text-align: center;">135.807</p> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p class="import-Normal"><em>Note. </em>The second column is in gray because this is what this book uses for all examples (i.e., tests with 95% confidence).</p> </div> 
	</div>
			
				
				
	</div>
<div class="back-matter glossary " id="back-matter-glossary" title="Glossary">
	<div class="back-matter-title-wrap">
		<p class="back-matter-number">6</p>
		<h1 class="back-matter-title">Glossary</h1>
								</div>
	<div class="ugc back-matter-ugc">
				 <dl data-type="glossary"><dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-alpha-">Alpha (α)</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The total area of the distribution that is in the tail or tails of a distribution.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-alternative-hypothesis">Alternative hypothesis</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Notated as <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=SF9h&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="H_a" title="H_a" class="latex mathjax" />; a statement of difference.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-bar-charts">Bar charts</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Charts in which the height of the bars conveys the frequency within categories.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-bivariate-statistics">Bivariate statistics</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Analyses that examine differences and relationships between two variables.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-central-limit-theorem">Central limit theorem</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Tenets pertain to shape (i.e., as sample size increases, the shape of the sampling distribution of <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x}" title="\bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" /> approximates normality), mean (i.e., the mean of the sampling distribution equals the mean in the population from which samples were randomly selected), and variability (i.e., the standard deviation of the sampling distribution decreases as the sample size increases).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-central-tendency">Central tendency</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The most typical value in a data set; can be found with an average (mean), by finding the middle or midpoint in a data set (median), or by finding the most common value (mode).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-chi-square">Chi-square</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Examines frequency distributions when you have nominal (categorical) data.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-chi-square-goodness-of-fit-test">Chi-square goodness-of-fit test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A test that examines a univariate frequency distribution for a nominal variable.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-chi-square-test-of-independence">Chi-square test of independence</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Tests bivariate frequency distributions of nominal variables.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-confidence">Confidence</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The total area of the distribution that is not in the tail or tails of a distribution.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-confidence-intervals">Confidence intervals</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Give us a range within which the population parameter is located (with 95% or 99% confidence).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-correlation">Correlation</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Degree of the linear relationship between two continuous variables.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-cutoff-scores">Cutoff scores</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>To find cutoff scores, you start with the percentage you are interested in and work backward to find the z-score and raw score (x) that are associated with the top or bottom percentage.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-dependent-samples-t-test">Dependent samples t-test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Also known as a paired samples t-test; a dependent samples t-test is when you compare two dependent sample means.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-dependent-variable">Dependent variable</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>What is affected or changed by the independent variable. The dependent variable is what is hypothesized to vary or change depending on the independent variable. Also known as an outcome variable.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-descriptive-analyses">Descriptive Analyses</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Descriptive parameters and statistics are used to organize, summarize, and communicate information about your data.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-deviation-scores">Deviation scores</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Tell you how far away each value is from the mean; you can see how little or how much the scores deviate from the mean.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-directional-hypothesis">Directional hypothesis</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Also referred to as a one-tailed test; you have just one alpha area, or one tail, that you are testing with your hypothesis.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-distributions">Distributions</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Show us how groups of values are organized.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-evidence-based-practices">Evidence-based practices</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Integrating the best research evidence, clinical expertise, and participant and community values into our work.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-figures">Figures</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Charts, pictures, or graphs that are used to help display and describe data.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-frequency-distributions">Frequency distributions</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Group together cases with the same values.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-histograms">Histograms</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>In a histogram, the height of the bar shows the frequency of each category or value of your variable. Because histograms display continuous variables, the bars of the graph are right next to each other, or the bars are touching.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-hypothesis">Hypothesis</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>An informed assumption or prediction about your research question.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-hypothesis-testing">Hypothesis testing</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A step-by-step process in testing your research hypothesis with an inferential statistics test.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-independent-samples-t-test">Independent samples t-test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Compares means in two samples, each randomly selected from a different population.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-independent-variable">Independent variable</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>What causes change or affects the other variable. Also known as a predictor variable.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-inferential-analyses">Inferential analyses</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Inferential statistics use samples to draw conclusions about a population using specific tests.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-inferential-statistics">Inferential statistics</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Allow us to draw conclusions about populations based on samples randomly selected (i.e., sampled by methods of chance, such as drawing names from a hat) from these populations.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-interval">Interval</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>With interval variables, we can classify a variable’s values into order, and we also know the interval or distance between values. The equal, known distances between numbers allow us to add and subtract; however, there is no true zero point (i.e., zero does not represent the absence of what we are measuring) with interval variables.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-kurtosis">Kurtosis</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Relates to the “peakedness” of a distribution, which reflects the weight of the distribution’s tails.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-leptokurtic-shape">Leptokurtic shape</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>When a distribution has high levels of kurtosis.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-level-or-scale-of-measurement">Level (or scale) of measurement</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The ways in which values of a variable are expressed.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-linear-regression">Linear regression</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The goal is to make a prediction, using the linear equation.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-mean">Mean</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The value found by calculating the average of a data set (i.e., taking the sum of all values, then dividing by the number of cases or people in your population or sample). Must have an interval or ratio level of measurement.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-median">Median</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The value at the midpoint in an ordered data set (the middle).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-mesokurtic-shape">Mesokurtic shape</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Shape of a normal distribution.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-mode">Mode</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The value or values in a data set that are the most frequently occurring (the most common value[s]).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-multivariate-statistics">Multivariate statistics</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Analyses that examine differences and relationships between three or more variables.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-needs-assessments">Needs assessments</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Gather or collect data to understand the need for services and support for a population in a service region or setting.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-negative-correlation">Negative correlation</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Pearson’s r value between –1 and &lt;0; these variables move in the opposite direction.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-no-correlation">No correlation</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Pearson’s r value of 0; no meaningful relationship between the two variables.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-nominal">Nominal</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Numerals are assigned to categorize (label) various characteristics or attributes. These assigned numerals are meaningless or arbitrary and are simply used to organize and summarize information about this variable.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-nonparametric-analysis">Nonparametric analysis</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Data do not meet assumptions (e.g., data are not a normal distribution with continuous data) of parametric analyses.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-normal-distribution">Normal distribution</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Also referred to as the <em>bell-shaped curve</em> because of its shape, the normal distribution is a theoretical distribution. This means that the distribution resulted from mathematical principles rather than actual data. Thus, it provides a visual and the key features of how a variable is expected to look (i.e., be distributed). In statistics, we can use this theoretical model and compare our actual data distributions to it; we can approximate it.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-null-hypothesis">Null hypothesis</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Notated as <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=SF9v&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="H_o" title="H_o" class="latex mathjax" />; a statement of no difference.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-one-sample-t-test">One-sample t-test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Comparing one sample mean to the mean of a population.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-operationalization">Operationalization</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Process of translating a variable into measurable terms.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-ordinal">Ordinal</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Organizing values with rank ordering. This allows us to classify or organize values again and allows us to put those categories in a particular order.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-p-values">P values</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Probability values that are computed alphas.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-parameters">Parameters</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Numerical values used to summarize data from a population.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-percentile-scores">Percentile scores</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The percentage of scores that fall below the observed score.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-pie-or-donut-charts">Pie or donut charts</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Display frequencies of categorical values; the size of the slice conveys the proportion of individuals in a category.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-platykurtic-shape">Platykurtic shape</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>When a distribution has low levels of kurtosis.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-pooled-variance-t-test">Pooled variance t-test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The two population variances are equal.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-population">Population</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>An entire set of individuals we are interested in learning about.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-positive-correlation">Positive correlation</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Pearson’s r value between &gt;0 and 1; these variables move in the same direction.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-probability">Probability</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Assess how likely or probable it is that a result you obtain occurred by random chance.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-qualitative-methods">Qualitative methods</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Use nonnumerical data, such as text or audio from interviews, focus groups, or observations, to explore and examine themes and experiences.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-quantitative-methods">Quantitative methods</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Use numerical data to examine relationships and differences.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-range">Range</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A measure of variability that represents the distance separating the highest from the lowest values.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-ratio">Ratio</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>With ratio variables, we can use our variable values to classify, they go in a particular order, we know the interval between values, and there is a true zero point (i.e., zero represents the absence of what we are measuring).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-research">Research</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A systematic method of inquiring, exploring, examining, or investigating questions, with the aim of discovering new findings, demonstrating impact, advocating for change, solving problems, or advancing theories.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-residual">Residual</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The distance between the estimated line and each data point.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-sample">Sample</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A subset of individuals from a given population.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-sampling-distribution">Sampling distribution</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A theoretical distribution that results from taking an infinite number of random samples of the same size from the same population.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-sampling-error">Sampling error</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The difference between our sample statistic and the population parameter.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-scientific-method">Scientific method</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The process in which we test a research question; includes seven steps: (1) ask a question, (2) conduct background research, (3) generate testable hypotheses, (4) design the study, (5) gather data to test the hypothesis, (6) analyze results, and (7) draw conclusions.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-separate-variance-t-test-or-welchs-t-test">Separate variance t-test or Welch’s t-test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Test that does not assume the population variances are equal.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-skew">Skew</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The amount to which a distribution departs from being symmetrical as a result of being pulled by outliers on one side of a distribution.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-slope">Slope</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Represents the average change in y for every 1-unit increase in <em>x</em>.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-standard-deviation">Standard deviation</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A way to measure overall variability; it removes squared units (the unit of variance is squared). You take the square root of the variance to find your standard deviation.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-standard-normal-distribution">Standard normal distribution</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Also called a <em>z</em> distribution; has a mean of 0 (at the center of the distribution) and a standard deviation of 1 (lines going above and below the mean at the center of the distribution are in increments of 1). If a <em>z</em>-score is positive, it is above the mean (of 0). If a <em>z</em>-score is negative, it is below the mean (of 0).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-statistical-analyses">Statistical analyses</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Mathematical techniques used to organize, summarize, and interpret numerical data obtained from assessments, reports, research studies, or evaluations.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-statistics">Statistics</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Numerical values used to summarize data from a sample.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-sum-of-squares">Sum of squares</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Squared deviation scores (which represent how far each value is from the mean) used to measure overall variability.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-t-distribution">T distribution</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Looks very similar to the standard normal distribution. Area of 1.00 under the curve; the mean is at 0. If your sample size is around 100 or greater, the shape of your distribution looks normal. The shape changes slightly as a function of the degrees of freedom.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-t-test">T-test</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Allows us to test inferential hypotheses with smaller samples and when we do not know our population standard deviation.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-type-i-error">Type I error</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>When you say there is a difference, but there actually isn’t a difference. Also referred to as a <em>false positive</em>.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-type-ii-error">Type II error</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>When you say there is not a difference, but there actually is a difference. Also referred to as a <em>false negative</em>.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-univariate-statistics">Univariate statistics</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Analyses that summarize numerical information about a single variable.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-variability">Variability</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A value that describes the dispersion or spread of values around a point of central tendency.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-variable">Variable</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>Any observation of a physical attribute, attitude, behavior, characteristic, mood, experience, and so forth that can take on different values.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-variance">Variance</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A way to measure overall variability; it accounts for the size of your population or sample by dividing your sum of squares value by <em>N</em>.</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-y-intercept">Y-intercept</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>The predicted value of y when x is equal to 0 (i.e., when the line crosses the y-axis).</p> </dd> <dt data-type="glossterm"><dfn id="dfn-z-score">Z-score</dfn></dt> <dd data-type="glossdef"><p>A standardized score calculated by taking a raw score (x), subtracting the mean of the population or sample you are comparing it to (mu or <img src="https://mathjax.library.illinois.edu/latex?latex=XGJhcnt4fQ&amp;fg=000000&amp;svg=1&amp;isBase64=1" alt="\bar{x}" title="\bar{x}" class="latex mathjax" />), and dividing by the population or sample standard deviation.</p> </dd> </dl> 
	</div>
			
				
				
	</div>
